WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.668
- There was a moment,

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.203
probably, we were down.

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:06.006
It was a back-and-forth game, ish,
but they definitely had the lead.

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.508
And there was a moment where I was like,
this might be your last half,

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.010
so let's fucking go, you know?

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.446
- [Taylor Rooks:] Wow.
- Like, I actually thought that to myself.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.558
- Oh, Sue Bird is

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:30.430
in the house!
Which, I recently learned,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.632
it was almost me
saying Courtney Bird.

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:33.366
- Oh yeah.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:36.302
- Your name was al— [Laughing]
- You've done your research.

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:37.404
You've done your research.

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:39.839
- Courtney Bird, like, doesn't even sound right.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:40.907
- I know. Courtney Elizabeth.

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.976
- Oh, I don't know about that.

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:43.543
- Courtney Elizabeth.

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.446
- So which parent was Team Courtney?

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.181
- God, that's a good question.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:49.849
I want to say—I actually don't know.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.918
I think they were both,
it was like a joint,

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.820
yeah, a joint almost decision.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.521
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:55.221
You know, I love, like,
how Megan [Rapinoe] said

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:59.192
you're Sue to the core, like 100 percent.
Like, your name is Sue Bird.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.126
It cannot be—

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:00.727
- It's one word.
- Anything else.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:03.930
- Yeah, it's one word, yeah.
- Yeah, it suits you. And it's Sue Brigit Bird?

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.231
- Yeah, Brigit.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:05.865
- Aw, I love that.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:06.666
Yeah, I heard that.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.902
I'm like, that's probably the best decision—
- [Laughing]

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:11.971
- Your parents ever made
in your whole life

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:13.106
is making sure that you were Sue Bird
and not Courtney Bird.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.074
I love it.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:16.209
They love you here in Seattle.
- [Laughing]

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.945
- Like, every person that has asked me
why I'm in Seattle and I say,

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:21.648
"Oh, I'm here to interview Sue Bird."
They all freak out.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.851
- [Laughing]
- I mean, do you feel that Seattle love all the time?

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.220
- Um, yeah. They're pretty amazing out here.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:27.954
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:30.557
It's like the perfect sports city.

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:34.327
They love their sports.
It's like a big small city.

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:37.697
Big enough where you get good restaurants,
you get good shopping,

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.066
but small enough where it's like,
kind of cozy in that way.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:41.568
- Yeah. Absolutely.
- Yeah.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:45.672
- Well, you know, I got to watch UConn
game here in my hotel.

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:46.005
- OK.
- OK, what is it like

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:49.609
watching a tournament game [laughing]

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:50.777
for UConn with Sue Bird?

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:52.479
- Uh, you know, when I'm in,

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:55.381
like, the privacy
of my own home, I definitely get into it.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:55.782
I'm, like, up and down

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:59.486
and over here and over there
and jumping and yelling and cheering.

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.889
My hand actually hurts
from Megan's high-fives.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.524
I was like, babe, you gotta take your rings off.
- [Laughing]

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:07.660
- You're actually—it's like starting to hurt
cuz she gets into it.

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.363
And that game was like, instant classic.

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.765
So there were so many highs and lows.
- Mmhmm.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:15.401
- So as a fan, it was really fun to watch.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:17.537
Forget UConn winning, losing.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.539
NC State easily could have won that.
Just as like a fan,

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:20.406
that was such a great game.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.242
- Yeah, cuz that was
the thing about that game.

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.810
And I said it was like, UConn played great,

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.412
NC State played great,
but then we just ran out of time.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.113
- Ran out of time.

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:31.084
- I mean, it was just
UConn happened to outlast them.

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:33.686
Paige Bueckers is so good.
- [Laughing]

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.755
- What do you see when you look at Paige?

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:44.097
- Um, God. I see, like, an endless ceiling,
just like literally

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:47.934
definition of sky's the limit
because she's clearly talented, right?

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.669
She definitely has that, like,

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:55.074
clutch gene, you know,
game's on the line, doesn't care.

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.610
Just goes at—although in regulation,
I was like, Paige, go get the ball.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.645
What are you doing?

00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:00.647
I don't know if anybody else felt that way.
- Yeah. [Laughing]

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.016
- And then luckily, she turned it on in overtime.
- Yeah.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:06.586
- But yeah, she has like all of those things
that I feel like a lot of us

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:09.189
athletes get asked about. Like, how—

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.057
so, how does one get the clutch gene?

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:14.294
Or, how does one, you know,
thrive in late-game situations?

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:15.762
There's really no answer.

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:17.397
Sometimes it's just that you have it
or you don't.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:20.133
- Yeah.
- And she has it, but obviously, she's young.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.302
So that's why I say that
about the ceiling.

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:24.270
Like, the ceiling is the roof, you know.
- Totally.

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:25.605
[Both laughing]

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:26.973
Shoutout MJ.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:27.907
But no, I agree.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:30.810
That's the thing with her.
It is so innate.

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:34.214
And when you think about that game
and, you know, some others,

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:35.515
the only thing that for her

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:38.718
probably working on is just,
I'm the person that has to take over

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:41.754
and like her kind of realizing it
cuz that's what I thought

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:42.655
when I watched the game too.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.425
Like, this is the moment
you have to go be Paige.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:48.027
And she did it, but she did it eventually.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:48.695
Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:52.865
And I do—I mean, you have to give her
credit too. Like, had midseason surgery,

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:56.703
trying to get her everything, like her mojo—
- Yeah.

00:03:56.703 --> 00:04:00.206
- Her vibe, her health back,
so it's not an easy moment.

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:01.908
So hopefully,

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.510
hopefully, this kind of propels her
for the Final Four

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.845
because that's not gonna be an easy game.

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:06.446
- Yeah.

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:09.649
- And Dorka [Juhász] with her wrist. [Gagging]
- Bad, yeah.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.185
That was just so sad.
- That was disgust—oh my God, that was tough.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:13.920
- Yeah.
- So that's gonna hurt them.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.289
So they're gonna need everybody
to step up.

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:18.858
And Paige, like I said,
hopefully this sets her off.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:20.426
- What does Paige do better than you?

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:22.829
Better than that UConn you?
- Oh God.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:23.429
Probably everything—
- Yeah.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.466
- Better than me.
Um, we're totally different. Yeah.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:31.704
She—honestly, if I were to pick one thing,
her confidence is just crazy.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:34.607
And it's not that I'm not confident,
but I think, like, all athletes

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.276
at different points
can struggle with that.

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:37.644
It's very commonplace.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:40.380
Whereas she feels—
she's more similar to Diana [Taurasi] in that way.

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:43.750
And I'm not comparing them,
but that's like something I see similar.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:47.287
It's just this, this,
this confidence that comes from—

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:50.256
comes from what? Comes from doing it
or you just have it, but it's there.

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:53.026
- Yeah. It's just like this swag.
- Yes.

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:54.861
- They're unfazed.

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:55.695
Yeah, absolutely.
- Right.

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:56.562
- It's interesting to hear

00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:59.565
you talk about confidence
and you say that is a thing that, like,

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:01.868
you have struggled with and athletes struggle with.
- Of course.

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:04.537
- What—give me an example
of what you mean by that.

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:08.207
- I mean, I think—oh God,
like a stupid one would just be,

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:12.078
you know, you have some players
go into games and whether they make their

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:16.316
first shot, make their first five shots
or miss their first 10,

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.351
they just keep going. They keep shootin'.

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:21.387
They have—they are unfazed,
like you just said, doesn't bother them.

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.756
I internalize some of that, right?

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:27.427
So if I come out—this is
hopefully not as much now,

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:29.996
but definitely when I was younger,
like if I come out,

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:33.466
miss a few shots,
I might be a little more, you know, tentative.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:35.101
Maybe I had a couple turnovers.

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:37.070
I might be a little more careful.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.504
I don't have, like,

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.907
that just blind confidence.
- Yeah.

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:43.176
- Like, I definitely internalize mistakes.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.644
Yeah.
- How do you get better at that?

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.147
- Whew. Million-dollar question.
- [Laughing] Yeah.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:51.384
- Um, I do feel really lucky that the way

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.920
Coach [Geno] Auriemma coaches you in college,

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:58.424
he essentially sets
you up to fail at all times.

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:02.795
So the more times you beat him,
the more times you beat the drill,

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:07.400
whatever's happening in practice, that's where,
like, that internal confidence comes from.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:11.938
And I think what happened
over time for me is I kind of have found

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:15.408
this place I can rest in, if you will,

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:18.678
this space of, like, law of averages,
almost, you know.

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:22.982
Like, listen, you've done it, you've proved it.
- Yeah.

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:24.016
- Clearly, you're good at this.
- [Laughing] Yeah.

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:24.851
- You know, like,

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:26.119
just rest easy in that.

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.087
Like, it'll turn around.
- Absolutely.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.757
- And of course, if you're thinking about it,
that's already a bad sign.

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:33.526
So you have to kind
of train yourself to just put all your,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.097
you know, for lack of a better,
all your eggs in the basket of,

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:39.832
I already prepared for this. I'm ready.
- Yeah.

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:42.201
- So even if you mess up a little,
you're still prepared.

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:42.568
- Yeah. Like everything that I need

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:45.972
is already in me because I have
done it time and time and time.

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:46.105
- Yeah.

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:47.240
You gotta trust that.
- Yeah.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.409
- So it's really about trusting that. Yeah.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:54.414
- You know, Coach Auriemma is clearly like
a huge basketball inspiration for you.

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:58.418
He's probably molded a lot of the things
that you think, you know, about the game.

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:02.355
He's a fascinating person to me
because it feels like

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:07.693
there is a very wide range
of opinions on Coach Auriemma.

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:09.729
- [Laughing]
- So I have a bunch of questions about him.

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:13.533
But what I first want to say
with the obvious disclaimer that he is

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:17.537
someone that you love and has been major
in your basketball career.
- Of course. Yes.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:21.407
- When you were in college, did you like him?

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:24.010
- It's a love-hate.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:28.247
I mean, I feel like everybody with
their college coach has these moments.

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:30.850
He just, he’d push. He's a pusher.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:35.121
And he really, he pushes you
to places that you didn't know existed.

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:38.224
But that's
when you figure out what you're made of.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:41.060
And so it's hard to hate it, you know?
But in the moment, yeah.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.896
Of course.
We always joke, like, we’d go to practice.

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:46.232
It's, like, the worst, you know,
two, three, four hours of your life.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:48.768
And then you go back to the dorm room
and talk shit.

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:52.138
And it's funny cuz we've—you know,
obviously you get older. Your relationship

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:55.074
with your college coach changes
cuz you're now adults.

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:57.610
And we've joked with him a ton, like,

00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:00.646
“Listen, just so you know, like,
all we did for four years is talk

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:02.248
shit about you
behind your back in our dorm room.”

00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:03.850
And at the same time,
I look at him and I'm like,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.953
“And we know all you did in the coaches
meetings was talk shit about us

00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:09.789
after every single day.
So we're on the same page here.”

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:10.623
- Yeah.

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:11.958
OK. No, that makes sense.

00:08:11.958 --> 00:08:12.925
So it was a love-hate.

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:15.228
And you understood that
all the things he was doing was

00:08:15.328 --> 00:08:18.364
to make you all a better team,
- Oh, yes. Absolutely.
- better player and things like that.

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:19.131
- But it wasn't easy.

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:23.202
- Yeah.
- Yeah. It wasn't easy.
- Because it feels like in a lot of ways,

00:08:23.803 --> 00:08:27.707
you are almost mentally
getting beat down there.

00:08:27.707 --> 00:08:30.476
I mean, is that accurate to say?
- Not far off. Not far off.

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.646
But, you know, it's like,

00:08:33.646 --> 00:08:37.817
listen, the stories that, you know,
are fun to tell, the memories

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:42.121
that all of us—when I say we, by the way,
I'm usually referring to, like, my class, right?

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:42.722
- Yeah.

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:45.525
- My generation.
Maybe, like, some teams I played on.

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:48.528
But the same goes
if I'm sitting in a room with Jen Rizzotti

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:50.029
or if I'm sitting in a room with Renee

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:52.798
Montgomery. Like,
we all kind of have this shared language.

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:57.937
And the best stories are the ones
where, like, someone got yelled at,

00:08:57.937 --> 00:09:01.908
somebody cried, somebody threw up. Like,
those are the best ones.

00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:05.144
But for every one of those—
and I have a great Swin [Cash]

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:08.414
one if we have time later—
but for every other one of those…
- We have time after you say this.

00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:10.516
- Um, she’ll kill me.

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:14.320
For every one of those, there's, you know,
three, four, five, six, seven, eight,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.356
nine, 10 stories where, you know,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.993
something happened where he lifted you up
or he had your back.

00:09:20.993 --> 00:09:25.531
I mean, one stupid one was I parked my car
in front of my dorm—like, we lived

00:09:25.531 --> 00:09:28.868
in these apartments my senior year—
and I parked my car in a handicapped spot

00:09:29.635 --> 00:09:30.970
by accident. Like, who knows?

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.071
I just wasn't thinking.
- Yeah.

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:35.908
- And we go on the road, and it was like,
get a phone call.

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.209
“Your car is in this handicap.

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:39.345
We're towing it.” And like,
I don't even know what happened.

00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:41.113
All I know is when I got back,

00:09:41.113 --> 00:09:44.050
he handled it. Like, the school,
like, the coaching staff handled it.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:47.219
And my
car was in the spot next to it.

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:49.288
Yeah. He was like,
boop, boop, took care of it.

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:49.722
- Yeah.

00:09:49.722 --> 00:09:52.224
- You know, so it's like,
there's moments like that.

00:09:52.725 --> 00:09:55.261
So there's—it's not even a balance.

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.362
There's more good than bad.

00:09:56.362 --> 00:10:01.133
- OK, because I remember hearing
you talk about a time you were there,

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:03.336
and he calls you in the office,
and he's like,

00:10:03.336 --> 00:10:06.138
Every bad thing that happens on
the court is your fault.
- Mmhmm.

00:10:06.872 --> 00:10:09.008
- Every good thing that happens is
your fault, but every bad thing.

00:10:09.008 --> 00:10:12.111
If this person messes up…
- I didn’t get credit for the good things.
- Just the bad things.
- Just the bad things.

00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:16.582
- Like, if this person messes up, if you're not
even on the court, like, it's on you.

00:10:16.616 --> 00:10:16.949
- Yeah.

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:19.151
- And I would think for a college player,
that is

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:22.088
an immense amount of pressure.

00:10:22.521 --> 00:10:23.322
- Yes and no.

00:10:23.322 --> 00:10:28.728
So looking back, like, in the moment,
I really didn't get it,

00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:32.264
like, in the moment, in that exact moment.
I was like, “What is he talking about?

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:33.532
That makes no sense.

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:35.401
How is this my fault?” You know?

00:10:35.401 --> 00:10:39.805
But now I totally understand that he…so

00:10:39.805 --> 00:10:43.809
what he's amazing at—and I
joke about this with him as well;

00:10:43.809 --> 00:10:45.945
I’m like,
“you're not the best X's and O's”—

00:10:46.245 --> 00:10:47.513
but what he's amazing

00:10:47.513 --> 00:10:51.317
at, like, truly amazing
at, is understanding people, understanding

00:10:51.317 --> 00:10:54.654
his players and understanding
how to get the best out of them.

00:10:54.654 --> 00:10:57.790
And it changes from player to player,
and he bobs and weaves with that,

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:59.825
like, better than anyone I've ever seen.

00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:03.396
So I think when he saw me or started
to get to know me, he saw a player

00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:07.600
that had that ability to kind of juggle
all of that on the floor.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.734
And kind of have

00:11:09.735 --> 00:11:12.805
my hands, my fingerprints on everyone
and everything.

00:11:12.805 --> 00:11:14.106
I think he saw that in me.

00:11:14.106 --> 00:11:15.207
And so that was him

00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:18.978
challenging me in a way to bring that out
because I do have a little bit of a

00:11:19.311 --> 00:11:22.214
shyer personality,
especially when I was younger.

00:11:22.648 --> 00:11:24.016
So I always joke, not joke.

00:11:24.016 --> 00:11:27.486
I always say it's like, “I'm a dip
my toe in the water” kind of person.

00:11:27.486 --> 00:11:29.588
Like, let me see
what's going on over here.
- Yeah.

00:11:29.588 --> 00:11:31.290
Feel it out.
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.726
- And he was basically trying
to speed up that process.
- Yeah.

00:11:33.759 --> 00:11:35.728
It's like, “No,
you actually have to jump in the pool.

00:11:35.728 --> 00:11:37.263
We don't have time for you to dip.

00:11:37.263 --> 00:11:39.131
We're trying to win championships.”
- Exactly.

00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:39.732
- Yeah.

00:11:39.732 --> 00:11:40.132
- Like, yeah. We don’t have time.
I got four years. That’s it.

00:11:40.132 --> 00:11:41.500
- OK, that's fair.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:43.302
That's fair.

00:11:43.302 --> 00:11:46.539
It's just there really is, you know,
there's the, the group of people

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:49.875
that feel like, OK, is the way
he's coaching people healthy?

00:11:49.875 --> 00:11:54.080
Like, is it a good way for you
to talk to people, you know, mentally?

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.948
Can you overcome

00:11:55.948 --> 00:11:59.919
maybe this, this environment
of what can feel like a pressure cooker?

00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:03.089
- Yeah.
- But it's always interesting
because everybody that leaves

00:12:03.089 --> 00:12:06.792
UConn is like, “Well, he changed my life
and he made me a good player.”

00:12:06.792 --> 00:12:10.696
So I feel like there's maybe some things
that are misunderstood about it

00:12:10.696 --> 00:12:12.498
because we are just seeing, you know,

00:12:12.498 --> 00:12:15.468
the press conferences or,
you know, what he says after a game.

00:12:15.501 --> 00:12:15.835
- Yeah.

00:12:15.835 --> 00:12:18.037
- You know?
- I mean, yeah,
those make me cringe sometimes, too.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:18.571
- Yeah.

00:12:18.571 --> 00:12:22.108
- But I know him,
and I understand there's a method

00:12:22.308 --> 00:12:24.076
to the madness.

00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:27.213
But I mean, I would say at the same time,
like, times have changed.

00:12:27.713 --> 00:12:30.349
People have changed. Kids are different.
- Mmhmm.

00:12:30.516 --> 00:12:32.485
- And that's not a bad thing, right?

00:12:32.485 --> 00:12:34.453
I see it in my WNBA experience.

00:12:34.453 --> 00:12:35.755
- Yeah.
- Like, I'm 41 now.

00:12:35.755 --> 00:12:39.458
So every draft class that comes in who's,
you know, they're going to be 21.

00:12:39.725 --> 00:12:41.360
I've seen it for the last, like, four

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.496
or five years
where it's like a little different

00:12:43.696 --> 00:12:45.030
and you kind of, you know,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:48.667
there comes a point I think with anyone
who is a leader in any position

00:12:48.968 --> 00:12:51.504
where you start to see
what's going around. Like, do you adjust?

00:12:51.904 --> 00:12:53.939
You know,
do you stick with what's always worked?

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:56.776
I think it's a tough question
as a leader.
- Totally.

00:12:56.776 --> 00:12:58.878
- Yeah. So I wouldn't be surprised
if he's thinking about those things.
- Yeah.

00:12:59.078 --> 00:13:02.715
And it's probably hard
when you are a proven winner.

00:13:02.882 --> 00:13:04.950
- Yeah. It's worked.
- Yeah.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.119
Like, well,
I know that this thing that I'm doing that

00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:10.189
you might have an issue with
has got me all these championships.
- Right.

00:13:10.189 --> 00:13:11.724
- And sometimes it's

00:13:11.724 --> 00:13:14.393
hard to tell somebody that
because they are like, “No, no, no,

00:13:14.393 --> 00:13:16.262
you don't know what you're talking about
because I'm the winner.”

00:13:16.262 --> 00:13:17.897
And I can see both sides to that.

00:13:18.931 --> 00:13:19.732
One more topic

00:13:19.732 --> 00:13:22.401
on him before we wrap up
the Geno conversation.

00:13:22.768 --> 00:13:24.336
You know, Candace Parker was on the show.
- My pants are noisy. Sorry.

00:13:24.336 --> 00:13:26.939
- It's OK. Great fit.

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:28.641
- Noisy pants.

00:13:29.375 --> 00:13:30.743
- You know, Candace Parker was on the show.

00:13:30.743 --> 00:13:31.243
- Yeah.

00:13:31.243 --> 00:13:34.814
- And she talked about her experience with Geno
and the Olympic team.

00:13:35.481 --> 00:13:37.616
And essentially saying that she felt like
she didn't

00:13:37.883 --> 00:13:41.587
make the team
because her and Geno did not get along.

00:13:41.887 --> 00:13:44.657
What was your reaction to that clip

00:13:44.657 --> 00:13:45.558
if you saw that?

00:13:45.558 --> 00:13:48.761
- Yeah, no, I saw it. Um, man, it's a tough one.

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:51.764
It's tough.

00:13:51.764 --> 00:13:54.033
I mean, I think if I'm Candace

00:13:54.033 --> 00:13:57.837
cuz obviously I was present
for all the things, right?

00:13:57.837 --> 00:14:02.041
Whether it's the, you know, training camp
where she balled out and got MVP.

00:14:02.041 --> 00:14:04.810
I was there. I was with her.

00:14:06.145 --> 00:14:07.246
They do an

00:14:07.313 --> 00:14:10.850
NBC kind of like huge media day.

00:14:11.083 --> 00:14:12.885
I was there with her for that,
you know?
- Yeah.

00:14:12.885 --> 00:14:14.987
- It's like, when you go to that,
you're kind of like, "OK,

00:14:14.987 --> 00:14:16.755
they must think I'm on the team."
- Yeah.

00:14:16.755 --> 00:14:20.626
So, and then I was— I texted her
when when they did announce the team,

00:14:20.626 --> 00:14:24.463
I texted her right away. Like, I don't even
remember what I said, but I just knew,

00:14:24.463 --> 00:14:26.232
Right?
- Mmhmm.
- Of course.

00:14:26.232 --> 00:14:30.236
And I think it's one of these things
where it's like, if I put my Candace hat

00:14:30.236 --> 00:14:34.540
on, I could probably sit here and tell you
that she should've been on the team.

00:14:35.074 --> 00:14:37.676
And then I could probably put my USA
Basketball hat on

00:14:37.676 --> 00:14:40.312
and be like, "OK, I understand
why this happened."

00:14:40.913 --> 00:14:43.916
- Mmhmm.
- So that is the difficult part
with our national team.

00:14:44.149 --> 00:14:46.085
It happened with Nneka this year.

00:14:46.085 --> 00:14:51.857
It's always going to happen
because we have that many good players.

00:14:52.157 --> 00:14:53.058
It's always gonna happen.

00:14:53.058 --> 00:14:55.327
As far as the Coach
Auriemma piece? I mean,

00:14:56.896 --> 00:14:59.765
he's the coach, he's not on the
committee, and it's, like,

00:14:59.865 --> 00:15:03.135
as an athlete who has been on the team
and who has been selected

00:15:03.135 --> 00:15:06.739
by the committee, I wanna believe that
the committee makes these decisions.

00:15:06.739 --> 00:15:08.974
It's almost like when you
have to believe in a system.

00:15:09.275 --> 00:15:11.744
Like, I'm choosing to
believe in that system.

00:15:11.777 --> 00:15:12.077
- Yeah.

00:15:12.077 --> 00:15:14.179
- And I'm choosing to believe
that it's working

00:15:15.014 --> 00:15:17.716
because we're winning.
Simultaneously,

00:15:17.816 --> 00:15:18.117
yeah,

00:15:18.117 --> 00:15:19.618
I mean, there's people
throughout the years

00:15:19.618 --> 00:15:21.887
that have been snubbed
that are my friends, and it's not easy.
- Mmhmm.

00:15:22.421 --> 00:15:24.924
- So it's like all these, it's
like one of those scenarios—

00:15:24.924 --> 00:15:26.859
and I think we're finding this
a lot in our society—where, like,

00:15:26.859 --> 00:15:28.394
all things are true.
- Yeah.

00:15:28.394 --> 00:15:30.095
Or there's some potential
nuance to

00:15:30.095 --> 00:15:32.765
the situation and it's not
so black and white.
- Yeah, all things are true.

00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:37.736
- Would you, I guess, understand
the perspective of somebody who believed

00:15:37.736 --> 00:15:42.107
that there is some sort of favoritism
that is shown…
- For the UConn players?
- Yes.

00:15:43.509 --> 00:15:45.144
- [Laughing]
- I mean, I always— so Megan and

00:15:45.144 --> 00:15:48.914
I talk about this a lot, the UConn bias,
um, trying to get to the bottom of it.

00:15:49.248 --> 00:15:52.184
- [Laughing]
- And she gives me an amazing perspective.

00:15:52.785 --> 00:15:56.922
Um, so what's very difficult
as a UConn player is

00:15:57.923 --> 00:16:00.259
I think immediately
when you're asked about this,

00:16:00.259 --> 00:16:02.494
whether it's the Olympic team
and making that,

00:16:03.028 --> 00:16:06.432
whether it's media coverage bias
that a lot of people talk about nowadays.

00:16:07.366 --> 00:16:11.203
Immediately as a player,
you think back to these four years

00:16:11.203 --> 00:16:14.707
where you busted your ass,
and you're kinda like, we won because,

00:16:15.708 --> 00:16:18.077
because we won. Like, the games
are the games are the games.

00:16:18.277 --> 00:16:18.677
- Mmhmm.
- Right?

00:16:18.677 --> 00:16:22.448
And the Maya Moores, the Diana
Taurasis, the Breanna Stewarts.

00:16:22.448 --> 00:16:23.282
I mean, think of these names

00:16:23.282 --> 00:16:27.219
I'm saying.
- Yeah.
- These are, like, I could've potentially named the top

00:16:27.219 --> 00:16:28.787
three players of all time, right?
- Totally.

00:16:28.787 --> 00:16:30.556
- Of course, you can throw in
many names to that;

00:16:30.556 --> 00:16:31.457
I'm not leaving anybody out.

00:16:31.457 --> 00:16:34.126
I'm just saying,
they're at like the tippy top.
- Yeah.

00:16:34.126 --> 00:16:38.831
- So like I said, from a player's
perspective, it's hard to understand.

00:16:38.864 --> 00:16:39.365
And I think

00:16:40.666 --> 00:16:40.966
you could

00:16:40.966 --> 00:16:46.238
also take a step back and understand that
because UConn has been successful,

00:16:46.905 --> 00:16:50.976
people are going to…
I was gonna say shine a light on that.

00:16:50.976 --> 00:16:51.777
I don't know if that's necessarily

00:16:51.777 --> 00:16:54.113
the best way to explain it,
but it's going to be covered more.

00:16:54.113 --> 00:16:55.547
It's going to be…

00:16:55.848 --> 00:16:58.217
and I see where that's,
like, hard for a lot of people.

00:16:58.217 --> 00:16:59.518
And I see—
- And probably annoying.

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:01.286
- Yeah, I'm sure it's annoying.

00:17:01.286 --> 00:17:03.455
I mean, yeah, I see it.

00:17:03.455 --> 00:17:07.059
But like at the same time, I'm like,
it's the best team. They've been—

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:07.426
forget that

00:17:07.426 --> 00:17:08.327
I'm UConn for a second.

00:17:08.327 --> 00:17:10.596
Like, it's the best team.
They've been winning.

00:17:10.596 --> 00:17:12.731
I mean, the amount of winning,

00:17:12.731 --> 00:17:14.500
it's really hard to argue that.

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:18.070
And I think the same goes for the players
that have been on the Olympic team.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:20.906
- Yeah.
- It's really hard to argue
these names.

00:17:21.373 --> 00:17:23.509
They're all-time greats.

00:17:23.509 --> 00:17:24.676
- Yeah. That's fair.

00:17:24.676 --> 00:17:28.013
And I like this, because
I think whenever there is a topic

00:17:28.013 --> 00:17:31.183
like this, it is important
to get all the perspectives.

00:17:31.183 --> 00:17:31.750
- Oh, yeah.
- You know,

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.086
the way that you see
it is gonna be different in the way

00:17:34.086 --> 00:17:37.689
that Candace sees it or Geno or another
UConn player or non-UConn player.

00:17:38.057 --> 00:17:42.928
And all of those matter to fully
understand, like, the scope of the topic.

00:17:42.928 --> 00:17:45.764
So I think that that really is true.
- I know it's a very politically correct

00:17:45.798 --> 00:17:47.566
I feel like what I said,
but at the same time, like,

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:49.268
it's

00:17:49.268 --> 00:17:52.504
like I said, I think when I
think about my four years and I'm like,

00:17:52.504 --> 00:17:56.675
I mean, we all were like, we all busted
our asked to win those championships.

00:17:56.675 --> 00:17:58.710
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, so it's hard to like…

00:17:58.977 --> 00:18:02.347
- Do you think that there is—
fix isn't the right word—

00:18:02.714 --> 00:18:08.153
but do you think that there is a way
for there to be more of a,

00:18:08.487 --> 00:18:12.691
I don't know, a confidence or transparency
in what happens with Team USA?

00:18:12.691 --> 00:18:15.527
Because it seems like
there are a number of players

00:18:15.694 --> 00:18:17.763
that have some sort of issue
with how it works.

00:18:17.963 --> 00:18:20.165
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:18:20.165 --> 00:18:21.700
It's like, I mean, I really don't know.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:23.969
There's 12 spots.
- Yeah.

00:18:23.969 --> 00:18:25.637
And a lot of good players.
- Yeah.

00:18:25.637 --> 00:18:28.240
From a transparency standpoint,
I'd have to think about it

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:32.811
because I don't know, like,
I mean, one thing maybe you could say

00:18:32.811 --> 00:18:36.115
is do you have more people
on the committee, right?

00:18:36.115 --> 00:18:37.316
More people…

00:18:38.417 --> 00:18:41.420
I don't know who those people would be.
But like, is that the answer?

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:42.421
Is it too small?

00:18:42.421 --> 00:18:46.492
Is it— you know, I'm like,
I really don't know because the—

00:18:46.725 --> 00:18:49.895
do you know the answer to all of this is?
- Yeah.

00:18:50.229 --> 00:18:52.331
Um, so Megan uses this "kiddie pool" analogy.

00:18:52.865 --> 00:18:53.866
It's basically

00:18:53.866 --> 00:18:57.936
like what we've talked about historically
with women and opportunity.

00:18:57.936 --> 00:18:59.605
And we've always said this pie,

00:18:59.605 --> 00:19:01.974
and we're only getting
this small piece of the pie.

00:19:01.974 --> 00:19:05.310
And the truth is, it's like,
in a lot of these conversations,

00:19:05.711 --> 00:19:09.681
the problem isn't that player X from UConn

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:12.818
is getting more attention
than player Y from another school.

00:19:12.818 --> 00:19:15.654
The problem is all of us
aren't getting enough attention.

00:19:16.188 --> 00:19:18.590
We all— It's like the,
opportunity for the attention.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:20.125
The coverage isn't big enough.

00:19:20.125 --> 00:19:22.060
So we're all fighting and clawing.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:22.528
- Yeah.

00:19:22.528 --> 00:19:26.832
- For the one article. For the one, you
know, I don't know, a story. For the one.

00:19:26.832 --> 00:19:29.535
And so it feels like we're competing
with each other the.

00:19:29.868 --> 00:19:33.872
So Megan says kiddie pool because we're
all over there fighting in the kiddie pool

00:19:34.206 --> 00:19:38.043
trying to, like, play with the one toy,
and there's this whole ocean.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:39.845
- Yeah.
- That's the problem.

00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:42.047
The problem is
we need to get into that ocean.

00:19:42.047 --> 00:19:43.282
So what this doesn't solve

00:19:43.282 --> 00:19:46.618
is the fact that there's 12 roster slots
because the answer is, "Oh, we need 15."

00:19:47.119 --> 00:19:47.753
- Yeah.

00:19:47.753 --> 00:19:51.023
No, I think that's a really
good way to put the,

00:19:51.123 --> 00:19:54.826
the problem in a lot of ways
is the system.
- Yes.

00:19:54.993 --> 00:19:56.595
And since we are

00:19:56.595 --> 00:20:00.098
all kind of living within the system,
you think that the problem is each other,

00:20:00.566 --> 00:20:03.202
and really are just
trying to make it work.
- Competitive.

00:20:03.502 --> 00:20:05.537
- Yeah. OK.
- We need, like, more.

00:20:05.537 --> 00:20:07.306
- Yeah.
- The answer is actually more.

00:20:07.306 --> 00:20:11.009
It's not that—and this is—it's
not that, you know—I'm trying to use

00:20:11.009 --> 00:20:16.348
some good names—
- [Laughing]
- It's not that Diana Taurasi
gets too much coverage, right?

00:20:16.682 --> 00:20:19.484
It's that Simona Augustus
didn't get enough. But

00:20:19.484 --> 00:20:22.254
it doesn't have to take from D.
She deserves that, right?
- Mmhmm.

00:20:22.621 --> 00:20:25.224
- It doesn't have to take from
Elena Delle Donne.

00:20:25.224 --> 00:20:27.359
She deserves it. It doesn't
have to take from A'ja Wilson.

00:20:27.359 --> 00:20:28.493
And she deserves it.

00:20:28.493 --> 00:20:31.964
It's just that there's players
this, this, this and this that also do.

00:20:32.164 --> 00:20:33.799
But there's not enough and we need more.

00:20:33.799 --> 00:20:37.569
- No, that totally makes sense
because there is no—

00:20:37.936 --> 00:20:41.240
there's not one person on the planet
that is seriously being like,

00:20:41.506 --> 00:20:43.742
"LeBron James has done
too many interviews."
- Right.

00:20:43.742 --> 00:20:46.245
Nobody says this about the men.
Yeah, yeah.

00:20:46.478 --> 00:20:49.748
And that's because it's like,
there's endless places,

00:20:49.748 --> 00:20:53.118
so nobody feels like the reason
he's getting it is because he's him and

00:20:53.118 --> 00:20:53.819
there's not enough.

00:20:53.819 --> 00:20:57.189
It's like, it's all so
there that it's not an issue.

00:20:57.222 --> 00:20:57.856
- Right.

00:20:58.056 --> 00:21:00.359
That is a, I think that's
a really good way to put it.

00:21:00.359 --> 00:21:01.860
And that was very striking to me.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.463
So I hope that people watching
feel like that is true.
- I think that that's what

00:21:04.563 --> 00:21:05.397
we're fighting for,

00:21:05.397 --> 00:21:06.632
to be honest.
- Yeah.

00:21:06.632 --> 00:21:07.065
Yeah.

00:21:07.065 --> 00:21:09.268
That's usually, like,
what's playing in my head

00:21:09.768 --> 00:21:12.571
just in any conversation
or interview or whatever.

00:21:12.771 --> 00:21:14.606
That's like, that's the goal.

00:21:14.606 --> 00:21:17.242
- So, do you—and again,

00:21:17.409 --> 00:21:19.978
because this is a system that exists,
it's not you specifically.

00:21:20.345 --> 00:21:23.048
Do you ever feel guilty

00:21:23.849 --> 00:21:26.818
about the coverage that you get?

00:21:27.085 --> 00:21:30.355
- Hmm. Sometimes. It's, like,
I don't know if guilt is the right word, um,

00:21:32.124 --> 00:21:32.791
because in women's basketball,

00:21:32.791 --> 00:21:35.360
there's so many variables, um,

00:21:36.962 --> 00:21:39.398
that come into play outside of the sport.

00:21:39.431 --> 00:21:43.568
Race being a big one.
Looks being another one. Right?

00:21:44.202 --> 00:21:47.706
Maybe it's, I don't know, a huge Instagram following,

00:21:47.706 --> 00:21:51.643
but you didn't even make a team
or something like that, right?

00:21:51.677 --> 00:21:52.944
- Right, mmhmm.
- Like, like a huge social media—

00:21:52.944 --> 00:21:56.648
but, so, there's all these things
that kind of, like,

00:21:56.648 --> 00:21:59.151
swirl in women's sports that don't,

00:22:00.218 --> 00:22:03.455
either don't swirl the same in men's sports
or what you just said,

00:22:03.722 --> 00:22:07.125
because there's endless opportunities—
- Yeah.

00:22:07.125 --> 00:22:09.761
- It can swirl. It doesn't matter.
Everyone eats.

00:22:10.429 --> 00:22:11.730
- Totally.
- Right? So it's,

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:15.600
it's an interesting—guilt?
Some, it's like, there's a sprinkle of guilt,

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:20.205
and it's more just having that moment
where you kind of take a step back.

00:22:20.205 --> 00:22:22.741
You look at what you're receiving, right?

00:22:22.741 --> 00:22:24.776
And you're kind of like, OK.

00:22:24.776 --> 00:22:28.180
Not about, it's not necessarily
about deserving or not deserving.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:30.849
I feel like my career
does speak for itself in a lot of ways.

00:22:31.383 --> 00:22:35.620
But also, OK, just because I'm white
or also, it's OK because I quote unquote,

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:39.124
look like the girl next door or—so,
you know, those questions pop up.

00:22:39.458 --> 00:22:42.027
And the reality is,
this is where I can use the platform.

00:22:42.027 --> 00:22:43.195
This is where I can use my voice.
- Yup.

00:22:43.195 --> 00:22:45.764
- This is where I can help
create more opportunities

00:22:45.964 --> 00:22:48.433
so everybody eats, for lack of a better term.

00:22:48.467 --> 00:22:49.334
- Yeah. Absolutely.

00:22:49.334 --> 00:22:53.805
And I mean, it's also really important
to say that so much of the platform

00:22:53.805 --> 00:22:54.406
that you have has been used

00:22:54.406 --> 00:22:58.810
not to shine light on you, but to shine light on everybody else.
You know, whether that's Togethxr—

00:22:59.077 --> 00:23:02.981
- Oh yeah.
- And you're giving stories to all girls

00:23:02.981 --> 00:23:05.183
and women that are playing sports,
whether it's using your voice

00:23:05.183 --> 00:23:10.355
to talk about Black Lives Matter
or equal pay or anything like that,

00:23:10.689 --> 00:23:14.226
you have used all the attention
that you get to bring attention

00:23:14.226 --> 00:23:16.194
to others that need it too.

00:23:16.194 --> 00:23:18.463
But yeah, I do think that's interesting
because, you know,

00:23:18.497 --> 00:23:21.767
sometimes you'll see people talk
about the coverage of women's sports

00:23:21.767 --> 00:23:23.268
and they'll say, OK, well,
you know,

00:23:23.268 --> 00:23:25.036
we only care about Sue Bird.
- Mmhmm.

00:23:25.036 --> 00:23:26.738
- And I get that.
But I also—

00:23:26.738 --> 00:23:29.508
- I'm like, who says that?
- [Laughing]

00:23:29.508 --> 00:23:32.177
But I also understand why. Like,
I don't think that that's wrong.

00:23:32.177 --> 00:23:33.478
It just goes back to
what you're saying about

00:23:33.478 --> 00:23:35.680
there should be more so that there is
the room to care about every single person—

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:39.050
- Right.
- Because everyone has a really important story

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:42.954
and they're bringing something, you know, to the table.
- Yeah.

00:23:43.388 --> 00:23:47.259
- And more than that, it's that, I think it's important
that you even have that awareness

00:23:47.559 --> 00:23:50.762
that maybe there are these other things
that are lending to it too.

00:23:50.762 --> 00:23:52.798
- Oh yeah, I mean—
- Yeah.

00:23:53.098 --> 00:23:53.698
- How do you not?
- Yeah.

00:23:53.698 --> 00:23:54.566
- Yeah.

00:23:54.566 --> 00:23:57.102
But I was gonna say the complicated part is, um,

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:02.941
the fact that we all have bias
and kind of trying to sort through

00:24:02.941 --> 00:24:06.511
where that comes from
or why it's there or, you know what I mean?

00:24:06.978 --> 00:24:08.747
Like, even a fan.

00:24:08.747 --> 00:24:10.015
So like, trying to understand—

00:24:10.015 --> 00:24:13.051
so it's just, I don't know,
how do you dismantle anything?

00:24:13.251 --> 00:24:17.255
I mean, it's a slow, long burn of a grind.
- Yeah.

00:24:17.656 --> 00:24:21.126
- But at the same time,
what gives me hope is kind of

00:24:21.126 --> 00:24:23.528
what's happened in the last
couple years in women's basketball.

00:24:24.196 --> 00:24:27.599
Just, it feels, like, it feels—
usually I'm just like, oh yeah, we turned a corner.

00:24:27.599 --> 00:24:29.534
I'm like, it's been a pretty dramatic corner
in a good way.

00:24:29.534 --> 00:24:31.069
- Totally. I agree. Yeah, yeah.
- In a very good way.

00:24:31.069 --> 00:24:33.138
So that's like, there's hope.

00:24:33.472 --> 00:24:34.673
There's a lot of hope.
- Yeah.

00:24:34.673 --> 00:24:35.974
- And like, I'm only saying
because you brought it up,

00:24:35.974 --> 00:24:39.945
but Togethxr is like, that's where, um,
I feel like I can't wait to be,

00:24:39.945 --> 00:24:43.915
once I do eventually retire, I feel like
I can pour so much more into that.

00:24:44.249 --> 00:24:48.620
And I'm so excited for that because,
A, it's just, like, fun to be able

00:24:48.887 --> 00:24:52.757
to tell these stories and just find out
about the stories themselves, you know,

00:24:52.991 --> 00:24:56.328
because even for me, it's like, it's
almost like this, like, unveiling of sorts

00:24:56.328 --> 00:24:58.897
where you're pulling back these curtains
and you're like, oh, dang, like this,

00:24:59.231 --> 00:24:59.865
you know?
- Mmhmm.

00:24:59.865 --> 00:25:02.400
- And so I'm really excited
because I know that can be an extension

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:05.971
of the sharing the platform
and shining lights and things like that.

00:25:06.271 --> 00:25:07.272
- No, I love Togethxr.

00:25:07.272 --> 00:25:09.074
If you're watching and you don't follow it, go follow.
- They do such an amazing job. I mean—ah.

00:25:09.074 --> 00:25:11.209
- I think the storytelling is great. Yes.
- So good.

00:25:11.209 --> 00:25:14.479
- They shine a—yeah, shine a light
on so many things, and you learn!

00:25:14.713 --> 00:25:18.316
Like, I learn about really interesting
and cool, fun athletes.

00:25:19.217 --> 00:25:21.319
And this is something that you and I

00:25:21.319 --> 00:25:24.589
have talked about before,
but Togethxr is really good at this.

00:25:25.257 --> 00:25:28.593
Not every single time we talk to a woman,

00:25:28.593 --> 00:25:31.897
we have to talk about the fact
that they're a woman.
- Thank you.
- Like [laughing],

00:25:32.664 --> 00:25:35.367
it's wild that this is the thing
that has any—

00:25:35.667 --> 00:25:39.704
for every woman that is doing something
that a lot of men do,

00:25:39.704 --> 00:25:41.506
I don't like using the term male-dominated

00:25:41.506 --> 00:25:43.575
because I don't necessarily
think it's male-dominated as much as

00:25:43.575 --> 00:25:44.543
I just think there's a lot of men.

00:25:45.677 --> 00:25:48.079
But you don't have

00:25:48.079 --> 00:25:50.982
to only talk to women
about the fact that they're women

00:25:51.149 --> 00:25:54.553
or how hard it is to be a woman
or they've overcome as a woman.

00:25:54.586 --> 00:25:55.186
- Totally. [Laughing]

00:25:55.186 --> 00:26:01.693
- And it's important for content that
includes women to be about what they do.

00:26:01.726 --> 00:26:02.961
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:26:02.961 --> 00:26:06.598
And so, I just really commend Togethxr
for doing that too.

00:26:06.831 --> 00:26:09.768
But how has that idea
kind of manifested itself

00:26:09.968 --> 00:26:13.138
in your life and your career?

00:26:13.338 --> 00:26:16.274
- Um, in terms of…
- Like only being seen as a woman.

00:26:16.341 --> 00:26:17.042
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:26:17.042 --> 00:26:21.413
- I mean, yeah, it can—it's a little bit
of an uphill battle at times.

00:26:21.413 --> 00:26:24.616
So there's a, there's balance
you have to strike between,

00:26:24.616 --> 00:26:27.719
you do need to talk
about some of these things, right?

00:26:27.719 --> 00:26:31.556
So like, I do need to talk about
some of these issues

00:26:31.556 --> 00:26:36.027
that women face, that women in the WNBA face,
female athletes face.

00:26:36.528 --> 00:26:40.932
And then I actually think because sports
mirrors life and vice versa so, so much,

00:26:41.032 --> 00:26:44.102
where even though I'm talking about
athletes, like, I'm talking about all women.

00:26:44.102 --> 00:26:46.504
- Yeah.
- I'm talking about all women in the workplace,

00:26:47.005 --> 00:26:48.540
like all women everywhere.
So there are things because, um,

00:26:49.741 --> 00:26:50.709
like the NCAA tournament

00:26:50.709 --> 00:26:53.311
with Sedona Prince
and showing the weight room.

00:26:53.745 --> 00:26:56.481
If you know that story—
if you don't know that story, like,

00:26:56.581 --> 00:26:58.683
you're probably under a rock.
- Yeah.

00:26:58.683 --> 00:27:01.786
- So I'm not even gonna explain it.
But what was so fascinating to me about that was

00:27:02.387 --> 00:27:05.690
we all, like, know this or knew this.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:08.727
Every one of us has experienced
that moment where you're like,

00:27:09.027 --> 00:27:11.296
oh, the men got that and we didn't?

00:27:11.796 --> 00:27:14.199
And yet it just took her saying it.
- Yeah.

00:27:14.265 --> 00:27:18.103
- So the lesson there is you have to say it.
Even if it's hard,

00:27:18.103 --> 00:27:19.337
even if it's uncomfortable.

00:27:19.337 --> 00:27:20.605
You have to.
- Yeah.

00:27:20.605 --> 00:27:22.807
- So the balance is you have to say it.

00:27:22.807 --> 00:27:27.312
You have to tell people these stories
because they, like, literally just don't know.

00:27:27.679 --> 00:27:28.246
- True.

00:27:28.246 --> 00:27:30.915
- Now, the reasons why they don't know
are the problem, right?

00:27:30.915 --> 00:27:33.385
Like how society is set up,
I guess, if you wanna say it like that.

00:27:33.985 --> 00:27:37.455
But then there is this other side where
it's like, I wanna come on a podcast

00:27:37.455 --> 00:27:41.159
and I want to—or a show,
and I want to talk about some fun stuff.

00:27:41.259 --> 00:27:41.760
- Yeah.

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:45.330
- Right? Because it's not, I'm not like just
this woman grinding away all the time.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.565
- [Laughing]
- Like, I do fun shit too, you know?

00:27:47.666 --> 00:27:49.768
- Yeah.
- And I have funny stories and like, you want to just be able to—

00:27:49.768 --> 00:27:50.802
and when I watch

00:27:51.770 --> 00:27:55.240
men or male athletes do these types of shows

00:27:56.441 --> 00:27:57.842
um, where they're,
where they're really just more so

00:27:57.842 --> 00:27:59.778
talking about like,
tell me about your life.

00:27:59.778 --> 00:28:02.547
It's never about if—they always
get to have the fun stuff happen.

00:28:02.547 --> 00:28:04.649
They always get to have the fun conversations.
- Yeah.

00:28:04.649 --> 00:28:06.785
- You know, maybe they're talking
about their favorite wine.

00:28:06.785 --> 00:28:10.455
It's like, sometimes I'll go on
different interviews and it's like,

00:28:11.056 --> 00:28:13.058
it's never gonna go to that place.
- Right.

00:28:13.058 --> 00:28:18.496
- And I exit those interviews
feeling like exhausted almost. You know?

00:28:19.097 --> 00:28:21.132
- Oh, 100 percent. Yeah.

00:28:21.132 --> 00:28:23.468
- I mean, like,
we'll get there. It’s getting there.

00:28:23.468 --> 00:28:26.738
I think we're just part of this generation
where we have to do both.
- For sure.

00:28:26.738 --> 00:28:29.941
And you're right. It is like this double-edged
sword of like, OK, it's important

00:28:30.208 --> 00:28:33.712
for women
to, you know, inspire other women, right,

00:28:33.712 --> 00:28:35.747
that want to do it. To
have them understand

00:28:35.747 --> 00:28:38.950
what it's like and talking about it
from the scope of being a woman.

00:28:39.284 --> 00:28:43.855
But there's also something to be said
about always being seen as a woman first

00:28:43.855 --> 00:28:47.192
and what you do second.
Because you work hard to be in this space

00:28:47.192 --> 00:28:51.830
because you are a world-class athlete,
you know? And everything

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:54.065
that surrounds
the coverage of female athletes

00:28:54.065 --> 00:28:57.569
is either about them being female
or it's about them, like, inspiring

00:28:57.569 --> 00:28:59.804
little girls, which both are important.
- Right.

00:28:59.904 --> 00:29:04.409
- But women don't exist in this space
in the service of others.

00:29:04.609 --> 00:29:08.146
And there's always this very weird line
that’s, like, teetered.
- Yeah.

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:11.249
- It's also like,
Well, you'll inspire little boys, too.

00:29:11.316 --> 00:29:13.251
- That is my, that's like my new, not new—
- Yeah.

00:29:13.251 --> 00:29:14.986
- That's been, like, my kick for a while

00:29:14.986 --> 00:29:17.889
where I'm like, of course,
I want little girls to see

00:29:17.889 --> 00:29:21.025
all of us and be like,
“Oh, I could be like her,” right?

00:29:21.226 --> 00:29:24.529
And then I want little boys to be like,
“Oh, this is normal.”

00:29:24.629 --> 00:29:25.597
- Yep.
- Right?

00:29:25.597 --> 00:29:28.466
Like, women in these positions of power
or women succeeding

00:29:28.466 --> 00:29:32.170
on the basketball court or in soccer,
whatever it is, like, “Oh, that's normal.”

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:33.438
- Yeah.
- You know, like,

00:29:33.438 --> 00:29:36.975
they're not having to at 18
learn about it.

00:29:37.208 --> 00:29:40.211
It's like they grew up with it.
And, yeah, I'm still toying.

00:29:40.211 --> 00:29:41.746
I was like, I did some speaking thing

00:29:41.746 --> 00:29:45.550
where I was like, little girls get to see
it, be it, and little boys get to see it…

00:29:45.817 --> 00:29:48.453
And I keep saying, respect it.
I don't know. I'm toying with this.

00:29:48.453 --> 00:29:50.755
- Oh, like, what's the right word?
- Yeah.

00:29:50.889 --> 00:29:53.591
Like, “see it, be it, see it…”

00:29:53.591 --> 00:29:55.593
- Ooh, we gotta workshop that.
- Yeah. We gotta figure that out.
- Yeah. Cuz that's true.

00:29:55.593 --> 00:29:58.129
I read this book
by an author named Kiese Laymon.

00:29:58.129 --> 00:30:01.866
And in it he talks about how
representation actually works both ways.

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:04.936
Like, in his example was with
Black people saying, you know,

00:30:05.336 --> 00:30:08.807
the thing that happens is
you can turn on a bunch of different shows

00:30:08.807 --> 00:30:11.509
and see varying
experiences of white people.

00:30:12.076 --> 00:30:16.681
But if a person can only see,
like, Black athletes or Black rappers,

00:30:17.215 --> 00:30:21.786
they have to actually see something else
to say, “Oh, this isn't just it.”
- Right.

00:30:21.886 --> 00:30:25.924
- So you're not just representing
Black people to Black people.

00:30:25.924 --> 00:30:28.726
You’re representing Black people
to non-Black people, too.

00:30:28.793 --> 00:30:29.093
- Right. Yeah, that makes sense.

00:30:29.093 --> 00:30:31.563
- And it's, you know, it's that same thing,
I think, for,

00:30:31.763 --> 00:30:33.097
for what you're talking about as well.

00:30:33.097 --> 00:30:34.999
I think eventually that will happen.

00:30:34.999 --> 00:30:37.602
We’ll get there eventually, but—
- The five-year-olds will become 25-year-olds that make decisions?

00:30:37.702 --> 00:30:40.371
- Yes.
- Perfect.
- That's the hope.

00:30:40.638 --> 00:30:41.272
That's the hope.

00:30:41.272 --> 00:30:43.741
And it's one of the reasons
I really love the commercials

00:30:43.741 --> 00:30:45.944
that you do,
specifically the one with Stephen Curry,

00:30:45.944 --> 00:30:50.448
because you are presented
as the standard in that commercial.
- Right.

00:30:50.448 --> 00:30:52.750
- And we don't see that a lot of the time.

00:30:52.984 --> 00:30:55.887
What went into deciding to do that
one specifically?

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:59.057
- Oh, I mean, it was—
I saw the script and I was like, “Yep,”

00:30:59.257 --> 00:31:01.659
because a lot of times
they show you the script beforehand.

00:31:01.659 --> 00:31:02.894
Obviously, you have to approve it.

00:31:02.894 --> 00:31:04.329
And if it's something wild,
you can change it.

00:31:04.329 --> 00:31:06.731
And I was like, “No, perfect. Let's go.”
- Yeah, I'm in.

00:31:07.232 --> 00:31:07.799
- Thanks, Steph.

00:31:07.799 --> 00:31:09.767
- Yeah, like I'm not the token here.

00:31:09.767 --> 00:31:10.535
I'm it.

00:31:10.535 --> 00:31:13.771
- Yeah.
- Like, I am who they're—even, you know, on the island,

00:31:13.771 --> 00:31:16.007
It's like, well, my blue is greater,
you know?

00:31:16.307 --> 00:31:17.242
All that's important.

00:31:17.242 --> 00:31:20.879
I love any Sue Bird commercial
that is about Sue being the best.

00:31:21.179 --> 00:31:22.614
Keep doing, keep doing that.

00:31:22.614 --> 00:31:23.281
- I love them because they're funny.

00:31:23.281 --> 00:31:23.748
- Yeah.

00:31:23.748 --> 00:31:27.485
- Like, that's what to me,
like, if you can—usually humor works

00:31:27.685 --> 00:31:29.854
in these wonderful ways anyways.

00:31:29.854 --> 00:31:33.157
So it's like, for me,
if you are presenting the female athlete

00:31:33.157 --> 00:31:34.425
as the standard, like you said,

00:31:34.425 --> 00:31:36.027
but you kind of do it in this,
like, fun, cheeky way.

00:31:36.027 --> 00:31:38.396
First of all, it's going to be memorable
because you laughed.

00:31:38.763 --> 00:31:41.366
And second of all, it's probably going to stick
and get the point across even more so.
- Yes.

00:31:41.366 --> 00:31:44.769
- So I love that all of these have had this,
like, humorous bend to them.

00:31:44.802 --> 00:31:45.236
- Yeah.

00:31:45.236 --> 00:31:47.739
And I hope it challenges people to realize

00:31:48.673 --> 00:31:50.775
men are always the default.
- Yes.

00:31:51.009 --> 00:31:52.810
- And that has to change.

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:56.714
You know, women are not like the
the shoot off of man.

00:31:56.714 --> 00:31:59.450
It's like, there’s woman and man.
You know, it's not like everything

00:31:59.651 --> 00:32:00.952
starts with men at the center.

00:32:00.952 --> 00:32:02.887
And if you're not that, you're
just adjacent to it.

00:32:02.887 --> 00:32:05.924
It's like these are two separate things
that are, that are equally as important.

00:32:06.557 --> 00:32:09.861
But to that point, I wanted to ask

00:32:09.861 --> 00:32:13.064
NBA players
if they had any question for you

00:32:13.064 --> 00:32:15.767
and wanted advice from you.
They all said yes.

00:32:16.067 --> 00:32:19.804
So I have a couple questions
from some NBA players for you.

00:32:20.805 --> 00:32:21.172
All right.

00:32:21.172 --> 00:32:24.375
First up, Kevin Love.

00:32:24.375 --> 00:32:28.379
Kevin Love says, “I'm curious
with Sue being the greatest of all time

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:31.449
how her approach to the game
overall has changed

00:32:31.449 --> 00:32:34.953
from when she came out of UConn until now.”
- Dramatically.

00:32:38.623 --> 00:32:41.359
I think with each phase of my career,

00:32:41.626 --> 00:32:43.795
there's been something I've had to change.

00:32:44.896 --> 00:32:47.131
But in the first

00:32:47.131 --> 00:32:49.200
10 to 15 years, I didn't really know it.

00:32:49.734 --> 00:32:52.804
So there was definitely a point,
let's call it…

00:32:54.138 --> 00:32:56.808
it's somewhere
between like the eight-, nine-, 10-year

00:32:56.808 --> 00:33:00.845
range of my career, where like
I needed to change, but I didn't know it.

00:33:01.079 --> 00:33:03.314
And so I kind of plateaued a little bit.

00:33:04.349 --> 00:33:06.918
And it took some surgeries.

00:33:06.918 --> 00:33:08.086
I had this period of time

00:33:08.086 --> 00:33:10.955
where I had like three different surgeries
in the span of a year.

00:33:11.255 --> 00:33:14.625
And that was the first time where
the physical really needed work. Like,

00:33:14.892 --> 00:33:16.728
literally my body of work needed work.

00:33:17.729 --> 00:33:19.497
And it
was in that moment where I was like, “Oh,

00:33:19.497 --> 00:33:21.466
I should have done this
like four years ago.

00:33:21.466 --> 00:33:22.667
I should have changed my diet.

00:33:22.667 --> 00:33:24.669
I should have worked out different.”

00:33:24.669 --> 00:33:27.138
And I think so my point is, like, now

00:33:28.006 --> 00:33:32.043
the approach is all about preparation,

00:33:32.243 --> 00:33:37.148
and what falls under
that is making sure I'm healthy.

00:33:37.148 --> 00:33:39.751
So all the things that fall under that,
some of them I just named,

00:33:41.285 --> 00:33:42.987
cuz to me, you can't,

00:33:42.987 --> 00:33:46.591
you can't perform at the highest levels
if something's off.
- Totally.

00:33:46.591 --> 00:33:49.227
- Now obviously, injuries come in.
Some things are out of your control.

00:33:49.727 --> 00:33:52.897
But, like, that has to be the priority,
for me anyways.

00:33:53.331 --> 00:33:58.102
And then with that, what that allows me
to do once those boxes are checked,

00:33:58.703 --> 00:34:04.208
now I can let what is really, like,
the key to my game

00:34:04.208 --> 00:34:07.311
and the key to me as a player,
and that's just letting my instincts

00:34:07.311 --> 00:34:10.181
take over, letting my mind
kind of do its thing,

00:34:10.181 --> 00:34:12.417
using my brain out there,
being the point guard.

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:15.319
Because I know the physical is there.
I can rely on that.

00:34:15.553 --> 00:34:15.787
- Yeah.

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:18.856
- So that's, like—my approach used to be, like,
nothing.

00:34:19.424 --> 00:34:21.692
Like, I was just going out there winging it.
- I’m just gonna figure it out.

00:34:21.692 --> 00:34:22.393
- Yeah.

00:34:22.393 --> 00:34:25.830
Now I'm like, oh, when this is good,
like, when this body is good

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:28.099
and I've taken care of it—
that's actually, to be honest,

00:34:28.099 --> 00:34:29.434
that's where all my confidence comes from.

00:34:29.434 --> 00:34:31.335
That's the best way to frame it.
- Really?

00:34:31.335 --> 00:34:31.636
- Yeah.

00:34:31.636 --> 00:34:34.639
When I can go out there and run around
and, like, cut and move and this and that

00:34:34.639 --> 00:34:37.508
and not have to think about it,
then I know I can actually play the game.

00:34:38.376 --> 00:34:40.945
And some of that has to do with my age
and like, you know, where I'm at,

00:34:40.978 --> 00:34:43.081
so it's not the same answer at 25 obviously.

00:34:43.081 --> 00:34:44.449
- Yeah.

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:44.749
- But yeah,

00:34:44.749 --> 00:34:48.386
my approach now is, like, all about making
sure I'm checking those boxes,

00:34:48.386 --> 00:34:48.686
- Yeah.

00:34:48.686 --> 00:34:50.688
- so I feel physically good.

00:34:50.688 --> 00:34:52.490
- It's interesting, though, because to me,

00:34:53.591 --> 00:34:56.928
what I'm thinking is you talking about
having to deal with confidence,

00:34:56.928 --> 00:35:00.898
but what you're describing is actually, no,
I already trust everything up here.

00:35:00.932 --> 00:35:01.732
- Oh, yeah. That's not a problem.

00:35:01.732 --> 00:35:04.502
- Yeah. Everything here
is completely confident, right?

00:35:04.502 --> 00:35:07.004
I can go out there and do that
any time, any day.

00:35:07.371 --> 00:35:10.741
But I need to make sure this is in check
so that that can perform at the level

00:35:10.741 --> 00:35:12.110
that it needs to.

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:16.214
- I got home from the Olympics
this past year.

00:35:16.214 --> 00:35:18.149
And, you know,
the Olympics is a little bit of a grind

00:35:18.149 --> 00:35:19.617
because it’s a lot of games.

00:35:19.617 --> 00:35:21.052
It's not a lot of time together.

00:35:21.052 --> 00:35:22.620
So it's, like, very stressful.

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:25.323
It's, obviously,
if you don't win a gold medal, oh, God.

00:35:25.456 --> 00:35:27.024
I mean, I don't wanna think about it.

00:35:27.024 --> 00:35:29.327
- Yeah
- It's very stressful.

00:35:29.694 --> 00:35:34.999
So, I got back from the Olympics, and
I think some of it was just, like, fatigue.

00:35:34.999 --> 00:35:37.602
Like, I was tired mentally, physically.

00:35:37.902 --> 00:35:41.706
But that last part of the season,
I felt my body go a little bit.

00:35:41.706 --> 00:35:45.843
And I think that's actually why—I'm
sure we'll get to retirement

00:35:45.843 --> 00:35:47.912
in a second—I think that's really why—
- We will.

00:35:49.147 --> 00:35:49.714
- At the end of the

00:35:49.714 --> 00:35:53.684
season, why the fans chanting
'One more year' really got me because

00:35:54.652 --> 00:35:57.421
I was really thinking about it.
Like, that,

00:35:57.488 --> 00:35:59.190
I think it was like a month. That month,

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:00.725
I physically didn't feel good.

00:36:00.725 --> 00:36:04.962
So it was, like, if I'm physically not
feeling good, I got nothin'.

00:36:05.296 --> 00:36:07.665
I'm not helping anybody, you know,

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:10.168
Which is the case for a lot of people.
- Yeah.

00:36:10.168 --> 00:36:11.502
- So it's not special to me,

00:36:11.502 --> 00:36:15.640
but that was really when the retirement
question first started entering my mind

00:36:15.640 --> 00:36:20.178
because I was just not—things
were just off, and it was kind of, like, oh.

00:36:20.711 --> 00:36:21.078
I feel like

00:36:21.078 --> 00:36:25.082
when you're 40, it's safe to ask yourself,
like, "Oh, is this who I am now?"

00:36:25.349 --> 00:36:26.484
- Totally.
- Like, "Is this who I am physically?"

00:36:27.885 --> 00:36:30.855
And that led to a conversation that I had
with my trainer in the offseason

00:36:30.855 --> 00:36:33.958
that I'm sure we can talk about.
- OK, trust me. There's a whole retirement bucket.

00:36:34.192 --> 00:36:36.561
So all of these will be asked.

00:36:36.561 --> 00:36:38.696
But, before I get to the next question
from a player,
- OK.

00:36:39.096 --> 00:36:40.765
- You said, 'I was really thinking about it.'

00:36:40.765 --> 00:36:43.234
I mean, like, quantify that for me.

00:36:43.234 --> 00:36:45.336
Like, what does that mean?
- I went into that—

00:36:45.369 --> 00:36:49.640
OK, so, this is, like, everyone,
but athletes

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:54.078
can compartmentalize, like,
nobody's—like, we're amazing.

00:36:54.078 --> 00:36:56.147
We're, like, literally experts at it.

00:36:56.147 --> 00:36:57.381
- [Rooks:] Yeah.
- OK.

00:36:57.682 --> 00:36:59.750
I was walking into that arena

00:37:00.985 --> 00:37:03.788
thinking—not knowing—thinking
it was my last game

00:37:03.788 --> 00:37:05.356
when we played Phoenix in those playoffs.

00:37:05.356 --> 00:37:07.291
I was, like, good chance.

00:37:07.291 --> 00:37:10.528
There was a moment—probably we were down.

00:37:10.528 --> 00:37:13.297
It was a back and forth game ish,
but they definitely had the lead.

00:37:13.297 --> 00:37:15.766
And there was a moment where I was, like,

00:37:15.766 --> 00:37:18.002
let's call it early in the second
half where I was

00:37:18.002 --> 00:37:21.305
kind of like, this might be your last half
so let's fucking go, you know?'

00:37:21.305 --> 00:37:22.974
- [Rooks:] Wow.
- Like, I actually though that to myself.

00:37:24.041 --> 00:37:25.076
So that's,

00:37:25.076 --> 00:37:26.177
I think that answers the question.

00:37:26.177 --> 00:37:28.279
- Yeah. Like you were. Yeah, it was there.
- It was right there.

00:37:28.312 --> 00:37:30.748
- It was right—wow.

00:37:30.781 --> 00:37:32.416
- I think I was just really tired.

00:37:32.416 --> 00:37:35.553
- Well, what's crazy about that story is,

00:37:36.087 --> 00:37:40.057
like, the fans literally brought you back.

00:37:40.157 --> 00:37:42.393
- I know.
- Cuz you were right there.
- Wild.

00:37:42.627 --> 00:37:44.962
- Yeah. Oh, my gosh. OK,
so much more to discuss with that.

00:37:44.962 --> 00:37:47.999
But I feel like these guys will kill me
if I don't get to their questions.

00:37:48.599 --> 00:37:51.202
Um, OK, so DeMar DeRozan—
you somewhat answered this.

00:37:51.202 --> 00:37:54.205
DeMar wanted to know,
"As you get older playing,

00:37:54.205 --> 00:37:57.508
what do you do to keep yourself physically
and mentally ready to keep going?"

00:37:57.541 --> 00:38:00.411
"Did you change your offseason
workouts, your nutrition?"

00:38:00.678 --> 00:38:03.481
You kind of answer the first part, but
did you change anything with nutrition?

00:38:03.481 --> 00:38:04.148
- All of it.

00:38:04.148 --> 00:38:05.483
All of the above.
- [Rooks:] Yeah.

00:38:05.483 --> 00:38:07.451
- I hired a nutritionist.

00:38:07.451 --> 00:38:09.220
Honestly, the one thing

00:38:10.254 --> 00:38:13.491
Stewie—Breanna Stewart—came to me after her

00:38:15.226 --> 00:38:16.227
second year, maybe.

00:38:16.227 --> 00:38:17.928
Yeah, after her second year,
and she was like,

00:38:17.928 --> 00:38:20.097
"If you were my age, what's the one thing
you would have changed?"

00:38:20.097 --> 00:38:22.433
And I was like,
I would have hired boop, boop, boop, boop,

00:38:22.433 --> 00:38:25.870
boop, and I would have gotten my workouts,
my nutrition, all the things,

00:38:26.037 --> 00:38:28.172
all the things.
I would have done that from day one.

00:38:28.606 --> 00:38:30.174
I think that the sooner you do
it, the better.

00:38:30.174 --> 00:38:33.577
When you're younger, you think
you can, like, get away cuz you can.

00:38:33.577 --> 00:38:35.713
- [Rooks:] Yeah.
- But why not start now?

00:38:35.713 --> 00:38:39.116
Like, why not start then and build, but
as the older player, one thing I would add

00:38:39.583 --> 00:38:43.154
to maybe, like, give DeMar
like an actual answer is less is more

00:38:43.154 --> 00:38:43.788
when you get older.

00:38:44.755 --> 00:38:45.589
- Mmm.
- Less is more.

00:38:45.589 --> 00:38:49.527
I feel, like, once you have in your own
mind established,

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:53.064
like, what kinds of, you know, workouts

00:38:53.064 --> 00:38:53.964
—let's use that as an example—

00:38:53.964 --> 00:38:56.300
what kinds of workouts
you need to be ready,

00:38:57.034 --> 00:38:58.569
at some point,
you have to have a conversation

00:38:58.569 --> 00:39:00.438
cuz it's probably,
as you get older, it's

00:39:00.438 --> 00:39:03.374
probably too much on your body,
and you have to figure out

00:39:04.375 --> 00:39:05.209
that balance.

00:39:05.209 --> 00:39:09.847
So, like, today. Today, I went to the gym, and
I have a clock, and I'm, like, start.

00:39:10.081 --> 00:39:11.749
And today, I shot for—

00:39:11.749 --> 00:39:13.784
Actually, today was, like, a longer day
and it was an hour,

00:39:14.452 --> 00:39:16.654
but I very rarely go over an hour
on the court.

00:39:16.654 --> 00:39:19.090
Very rarely.
- Mmhmm.
- Very rarely.

00:39:19.824 --> 00:39:22.426
- Very rarely.
- Don't know if you caught that, but very rarely.

00:39:23.327 --> 00:39:25.463
And even that, like—

00:39:25.629 --> 00:39:29.233
so, sidebar, training camps
in, like, very soon. It's in a couple weeks.
- [Rooks:] Yeah.

00:39:29.433 --> 00:39:32.103
- So, I'm in like full-on, ready
for training camp.

00:39:32.103 --> 00:39:32.737
So the answer to

00:39:32.737 --> 00:39:35.573
this now is different from a month ago,
but if I answered a month ago,

00:39:35.840 --> 00:39:36.841
I'm only on the court

00:39:37.808 --> 00:39:40.077
two, maybe three times a week.

00:39:40.077 --> 00:39:43.214
- Mmm.
- And like I said, very rarely
do I go over an hour.

00:39:43.314 --> 00:39:45.483
- Yeah.
- So less is more.

00:39:45.483 --> 00:39:48.519
Now, you want to be efficient,
you need to figure out,

00:39:49.186 --> 00:39:53.190
like, ask yourself what things do
I need to get done to feel confident

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:56.227
in my game, and then try to get those done
in an efficient way.

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:58.696
So you feel good, you feel confident,
you feel prepared, like you

00:39:58.696 --> 00:40:02.500
check those things on that list,
but you're not overdoing it on your body.

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:06.203
- Yeah.
- Cuz, the whole thing is
being as fresh as possible

00:40:06.203 --> 00:40:07.171
for when the season comes.

00:40:07.171 --> 00:40:07.638
- Yeah.

00:40:07.638 --> 00:40:11.909
And, I mean, I think understanding
your limit is an underrated skill.

00:40:11.909 --> 00:40:13.344
- [Bird:] Oh yeah.
- Just in general.

00:40:13.344 --> 00:40:13.544
- [Bird:] Yeah.

00:40:13.544 --> 00:40:18.649
- [Rooks:] People think they can do everything.
- Mmhmm.
- [Rooks:] But everything in moderation, even moderation.

00:40:19.417 --> 00:40:20.951
OK.
- [Bird:] Well, we all think we're, like, 21.

00:40:20.951 --> 00:40:23.254
- Yeah. I am still 21 actually, so.

00:40:23.254 --> 00:40:25.189
- People will be like, "Oh, you ran track

00:40:25.189 --> 00:40:26.056
in, like,

00:40:26.056 --> 00:40:29.527
you know, high school, like,
how fast could you run a mile?"

00:40:29.527 --> 00:40:31.762
And I give the answer I ran
when I was 21.

00:40:32.229 --> 00:40:32.696
- [Rooks:] Yeah.

00:40:32.696 --> 00:40:34.165
- I'm like, "Oh sub-six."

00:40:34.165 --> 00:40:36.333
- [Rooks:] Yeah. Years ago. Yeah.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:38.536
- Meanwhile,
I wouldn't be able to smell that now.

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:40.871
- [Rooks:] All right, next question.

00:40:40.871 --> 00:40:42.907
Got one from Ja Morant.

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:47.978
He said, "When she watches film,
is she focusing on the stuff that she does

00:40:47.978 --> 00:40:49.580
well or her mistakes?"

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:51.515
- Oh, both.

00:40:51.515 --> 00:40:51.916
Both.

00:40:51.916 --> 00:40:53.184
Probably more mistakes than

00:40:53.184 --> 00:40:56.253
well. I feel like if you're doing
something well, you already know that one.

00:40:56.253 --> 00:40:57.054
- [Rooks:] Yeah.
- Mistakes,

00:40:57.054 --> 00:40:58.789
there's learning to be had.
- [Rooks:] Yeah.

00:40:58.789 --> 00:40:59.523
- But I'll be honest,

00:40:59.523 --> 00:41:02.259
when I watch film, most times I'm watching
how the other team is guarding us

00:41:02.693 --> 00:41:07.364
more than anything.
- [Rooks:] Mmm. OK.
- That's where I can find moments to steal possessions.

00:41:07.565 --> 00:41:10.835
- But how did the way you watch film
change as you progressed in your career?

00:41:10.901 --> 00:41:13.170
- Oh, um.

00:41:13.204 --> 00:41:15.506
Yeah. Early on, I really didn't watch a lot.

00:41:15.506 --> 00:41:16.106
It wasn't like a thing.

00:41:16.106 --> 00:41:19.577
- [Rooks:] Really? Like, I was just going out there.
- Kind of. Like, we watched with our team.

00:41:19.577 --> 00:41:19.743
- [Rooks:] Yeah, yeah.

00:41:19.743 --> 00:41:22.079
- But, like,
no, I didn't go back to my—no.

00:41:22.079 --> 00:41:25.082
I mean, Synergy and all these fancy
things didn't exist, where you can, like,

00:41:25.082 --> 00:41:27.184
just type in stuff and go watch it.
- [Rooks:] Yeah.

00:41:27.184 --> 00:41:30.488
- I wasn't gonna like, you know,
watch a VHS. Dating myself a little bit.

00:41:31.755 --> 00:41:34.592
Um, but yeah.
I would say it was probably around 2010.

00:41:34.592 --> 00:41:38.295
We had an assistant, Jenny Boucek,
who's actually in the NBA with the Pacers,

00:41:38.696 --> 00:41:43.133
and we were going into our playoff run, and
that was the first time I ever sat down—

00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:44.401
this was with somebody.

00:41:44.401 --> 00:41:48.706
Now I do it up by myself—
and watched the games, and it was like,

00:41:48.706 --> 00:41:52.042
Oh, we can kill them running that
and we can beat them running this.

00:41:52.276 --> 00:41:53.644
And this is how they're guarding that.

00:41:53.644 --> 00:41:55.980
So let's do this and all those things.

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:58.516
And then I would make that,
like, mental list.

00:41:59.483 --> 00:42:02.219
Sometimes, I'd write it down, actually
keep it in my locker

00:42:02.219 --> 00:42:03.654
so I could, like, refer to it.

00:42:03.654 --> 00:42:07.491
And it was like, if I can steal two,
three possessions a game

00:42:07.491 --> 00:42:11.295
by just using what they're doing
against them, that's six points.

00:42:11.295 --> 00:42:12.263
That's a win.

00:42:12.263 --> 00:42:13.097
- [Rooks:] Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:42:13.097 --> 00:42:15.366
And that I've taken that with me since then.

00:42:15.566 --> 00:42:17.835
- OK. No, I love that. Shout out to Ja.

00:42:17.868 --> 00:42:18.435
Good question.

00:42:19.470 --> 00:42:20.037
All right.

00:42:20.037 --> 00:42:23.874
Devin Booker. He goes, "OK, Taylor,
so I know it's off topic,

00:42:23.874 --> 00:42:26.510
but I gotta know because she stays clean."
- [Laughing]

00:42:27.278 --> 00:42:30.247
- Who is your inspiration for style?

00:42:30.247 --> 00:42:31.549
- Style?

00:42:31.615 --> 00:42:36.053
I mean, I don't not like all his shit on
LeagueFits.

00:42:36.053 --> 00:42:38.455
- Yeah.
- I'll be like, oop, save. I'm like—

00:42:38.522 --> 00:42:40.624
- [Laughing]
- I pull from a lot of people.

00:42:40.658 --> 00:42:41.525
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:42:41.525 --> 00:42:45.796
Because I definitely like to dress
in a way where I feel comfortable,

00:42:45.796 --> 00:42:46.497
I feel good.

00:42:46.497 --> 00:42:50.401
And so if it's like a woman
who I see, boop. If it's a dude, boop.

00:42:50.401 --> 00:42:54.305
Like, I save on Instagram constantly,
so I'm always just swiping and looking.

00:42:54.305 --> 00:42:56.440
Devin, I for sure,
like I said, saved your pictures.

00:42:56.674 --> 00:42:57.508
And it really just depends.

00:42:57.508 --> 00:42:59.743
It's like what
jumps out and speaks to you.

00:42:59.743 --> 00:43:03.180
I do love, one of my favorite stylists is Courtney Mays.
- OK.

00:43:03.247 --> 00:43:05.950
- Ugh, I love Courtney,
and she does Chris Paul a lot.

00:43:05.983 --> 00:43:07.184
She does a lot of DeAndre Jordan.

00:43:07.184 --> 00:43:10.654
But so, Chris, every time, I mean, Chris,
when he comes in, I'm like [inhaling]—

00:43:11.822 --> 00:43:13.290
- Stellar.
- So good. So good.

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:16.160
- Yeah.
- So yeah, I don't have—I wish I had this like one inspo.

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:18.896
It's usually just like pulling
from different people.

00:43:18.896 --> 00:43:20.965
And I mean, shoutout to Megan.

00:43:20.965 --> 00:43:22.967
- Yeah. [Laughing]
- She's, um, she helps me.

00:43:22.967 --> 00:43:26.136
But more than anything,
what she did was, like, open my eyes

00:43:26.136 --> 00:43:30.207
to the possibility of finding your own style—
- Mmhmm.

00:43:31.008 --> 00:43:34.211
- That doesn't have to be necessarily
like anybody else's.

00:43:34.345 --> 00:43:36.113
- Yeah.
- Yeah. And it's like, what fits you?

00:43:36.113 --> 00:43:38.082
- I feel this way
about how Megan dresses, like,

00:43:38.082 --> 00:43:41.919
she has a distinct style, but
I feel that way about Devin Booker too.

00:43:41.919 --> 00:43:42.786
- Oh yeah, he definitely does.

00:43:42.786 --> 00:43:45.789
- Like, you see something and think,
that's something Devin Booker would wear.

00:43:45.789 --> 00:43:49.193
- Yeah. [Laughing] Totally.
- You know, which I, like—that's a feat to really have,

00:43:49.193 --> 00:43:52.363
like, your own very distinct
kind of calling card for style.

00:43:52.363 --> 00:43:54.365
So that was—
- You start with some Chucks. Then
you're on your way.

00:43:54.365 --> 00:43:56.567
- Yes. And the little, the pants,
you know, their lil'—
- Yeah.

00:43:56.634 --> 00:43:58.936
- You know,
they're not called that high waters,

00:43:58.936 --> 00:44:00.904
but you know how they're a little higher?
- Yeah.

00:44:00.904 --> 00:44:03.007
- It's—killing it, Devin.
- Cropped.

00:44:03.007 --> 00:44:04.041
- Yes. OK.

00:44:04.041 --> 00:44:06.210
Fred VanVleet, I was like,
this is a good question.

00:44:06.644 --> 00:44:11.015
He said three keys to making it to 20 years.

00:44:12.216 --> 00:44:16.353
- Oh. Man. Um. [Chuckling]
OK, so one key is what

00:44:16.353 --> 00:44:19.623
we've already said, which is the whole, like,
nutrition, taking care of yourself.

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:23.127
It's just like beyond important.
- Yeah.

00:44:23.127 --> 00:44:24.962
- Low-key, I wanna be like, good genes.

00:44:24.962 --> 00:44:27.364
I do feel like some of it's luck,
you know what I mean?

00:44:27.364 --> 00:44:31.035
Like, some of it is—
cuz I've had some serious injuries

00:44:31.035 --> 00:44:34.071
and, like, I've been able to bounce back,
so some of it is luck.

00:44:34.071 --> 00:44:38.108
I know that's a crappy key,
but some of it is that.

00:44:38.876 --> 00:44:42.246
I do think you have to be able to

00:44:42.379 --> 00:44:44.648
adapt and adjust.

00:44:44.815 --> 00:44:47.851
So, and this is probably
the most important, to be honest.

00:44:49.186 --> 00:44:52.556
I can't do what I did as a younger player.

00:44:52.556 --> 00:44:55.125
I just physically cannot move in
those ways.

00:44:55.125 --> 00:44:57.528
I'm not as fast, I'm not as quick.

00:44:57.528 --> 00:44:59.763
And I think a lot of athletes,

00:45:01.031 --> 00:45:01.498
when that happens—

00:45:01.498 --> 00:45:04.635
cuz it happens to all of us.
Like at some point, it happens.

00:45:05.002 --> 00:45:07.471
People still,
they don't want to give in to it.

00:45:08.539 --> 00:45:10.441
They don't want to accept it,
they don't want to embrace it,

00:45:10.441 --> 00:45:12.076
they don't want to adapt to that.

00:45:12.076 --> 00:45:15.412
So they just keep trying
to play the same way as they always did.

00:45:15.412 --> 00:45:19.116
And then they end up looking,
like, you can kind of see it.

00:45:19.116 --> 00:45:21.385
You know, they're like,
oh, they're in their old-person years.

00:45:21.385 --> 00:45:24.321
You know, it's like, oh, they,
they got old, you know?

00:45:25.122 --> 00:45:25.489
- Yeah. Your face [laughing].
Yeah.

00:45:25.489 --> 00:45:27.925
- It's like, cuz it's sad
cuz it happens to all of us.

00:45:28.625 --> 00:45:31.128
It's gonna happen—
it's like, it happens to all of us.

00:45:31.128 --> 00:45:33.030
But I think a big key for me is, um,

00:45:34.965 --> 00:45:37.067
I've been able to see those

00:45:37.935 --> 00:45:41.338
moments or those ways
in which things have changed for me.

00:45:42.372 --> 00:45:45.075
And it comes with a little bit
of a mourning phase.

00:45:46.343 --> 00:45:48.746
- [Laughing] Yeah.
- It's like, I used to beat my pull-up jumper—

00:45:48.746 --> 00:45:50.481
- You had a good run. [Laughing]
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:50.481 --> 00:45:52.149
Going right with that pull-up.

00:45:52.149 --> 00:45:55.085
You used to be the shit. Now…

00:45:55.085 --> 00:45:57.054
Especially, yeah, with my pull-ups,

00:45:57.054 --> 00:45:59.256
I don't have, like, the pop,
the one-two quick—

00:45:59.423 --> 00:46:01.125
it's just not the same.

00:46:01.125 --> 00:46:05.095
And there was a huge mourning phase for me
where I was trying to force it

00:46:05.095 --> 00:46:08.665
a little bit and I had to
kind of, like, let go of that.

00:46:09.133 --> 00:46:09.633
And I'm trying,

00:46:09.633 --> 00:46:11.135
I'm actually like currently trying
to like add

00:46:11.135 --> 00:46:13.370
something new to like
maybe make up for that.

00:46:13.370 --> 00:46:15.372
We'll see if it works. I'll let you know.
- Yeah.

00:46:16.573 --> 00:46:17.941
- Um, but that's like a big part.

00:46:17.941 --> 00:46:20.611
So you have to, like, accept
that you're not the same.

00:46:21.145 --> 00:46:23.347
Allow yourself
to go through the mourning phase.

00:46:23.347 --> 00:46:25.482
And then try to figure out another way.

00:46:25.482 --> 00:46:27.818
And just because it's not what you used to do
doesn't mean it's worse.

00:46:27.918 --> 00:46:30.554
- Yeah.
- Yeah, but it's like, you hold on to parts of your game.

00:46:30.554 --> 00:46:33.023
I think we all feel, like, really proud

00:46:33.023 --> 00:46:33.557
of these different areas of our game

00:46:33.557 --> 00:46:37.394
that we like developed and worked on
and like are ours, like signature stuff.

00:46:37.461 --> 00:46:38.929
- Mmhmm. Yeah.
- It's hard to let go.

00:46:38.929 --> 00:46:39.396
 - Yeah.

00:46:39.396 --> 00:46:42.432
You know, that makes me think of
when I was watching some of your interviews.

00:46:42.699 --> 00:46:44.034
This is, I mean, an interview

00:46:44.034 --> 00:46:45.869
you did years ago
and you are talking about

00:46:45.869 --> 00:46:48.405
when you started playing basketball
and you knew, you know,

00:46:48.405 --> 00:46:50.340
you weren't gonna be, like,
the biggest, right?

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:53.577
So you said you had
to find other ways to win.

00:46:54.545 --> 00:46:57.114
But now you're still doing that
same thing, like you're having

00:46:57.114 --> 00:47:00.717
to find other ways to win
and like, still be Sue Bird.

00:47:00.951 --> 00:47:04.087
Maybe not the Sue that we saw,
you know, all those years ago,

00:47:04.087 --> 00:47:07.457
but it's still at its core Sue
and how you're switching that up.

00:47:07.457 --> 00:47:09.693
So I think that's
really interesting.

00:47:09.693 --> 00:47:11.061
The last one is from Donovan Mitchell.

00:47:11.061 --> 00:47:14.198
He just wanted to know—
- Greenwich. Shoutout.

00:47:14.798 --> 00:47:19.236
- Yes. He just said, how do you keep the same
mindset night in and night out?

00:47:19.903 --> 00:47:20.537
- Whew.
- Yeah.

00:47:20.537 --> 00:47:22.639
- I mean, honestly,
I should be asking the NBA guys that

00:47:22.639 --> 00:47:26.443
with the 80-plus games
they play. That is just,

00:47:26.443 --> 00:47:30.914
I can only imagine that some nights you're
kinda like, can't find it tonight.

00:47:31.281 --> 00:47:32.416
- Yeah.
- Yeah. It's hard.

00:47:33.750 --> 00:47:35.986
Um, I sometimes trick myself.

00:47:35.986 --> 00:47:38.455
I…this sounds crazy,

00:47:38.455 --> 00:47:39.723
I can't believe I'm saying it out loud,

00:47:39.723 --> 00:47:41.491
I've only said it out loud like one other time.

00:47:41.491 --> 00:47:42.960
I will literally—

00:47:42.960 --> 00:47:46.330
so right now, I have found
crazy inspiration in my nieces.

00:47:47.297 --> 00:47:49.299
They're nine and seven.

00:47:49.299 --> 00:47:51.702
They're like,
they're starting to understand

00:47:51.702 --> 00:47:55.806
what's happening, like, with me
and basketball and, like, what's going on.

00:47:55.806 --> 00:47:57.674
They watched the UConn game last night.
- [Laughing]

00:47:57.674 --> 00:47:59.543
- They were very excited
wearing my jersey,

00:47:59.543 --> 00:48:00.077
which is hilarious.

00:48:01.411 --> 00:48:05.082
And I'm, sometimes, when I'm not in the mood
or I can't find it, I'm like,

00:48:05.082 --> 00:48:07.985
if they were in the building, like,
what would you be like right now?

00:48:08.585 --> 00:48:09.019
You know?

00:48:09.019 --> 00:48:12.022
And sometimes it is mind over matter.

00:48:12.022 --> 00:48:13.891
I think any of these guys
will tell you that, especially

00:48:13.891 --> 00:48:18.028
in like, situations, like a back-to-back.
It's mind over matter, right?

00:48:18.028 --> 00:48:19.696
And it's like, one team's gonna let it get to them.

00:48:19.696 --> 00:48:21.765
The other team's not.
And that's like the end.

00:48:21.765 --> 00:48:23.467
That's like how it's gonna happen.

00:48:23.467 --> 00:48:27.271
So I just literally will find ways
to, like, trick myself.

00:48:27.271 --> 00:48:29.806
And then what I find is like this
like adrenaline rush

00:48:29.806 --> 00:48:31.308
that kind of gets me over the hump.

00:48:31.308 --> 00:48:32.209
And then once you like—
- Really?

00:48:32.209 --> 00:48:34.444
- Yeah, once you kind of get over it,
over that hump,

00:48:35.145 --> 00:48:37.180
it just kind of like, you coast from there
in a good way.

00:48:37.547 --> 00:48:40.851
- So you're like, if my nieces and nephews were in here—
- I know, it sounds like Jedi mind tricks.

00:48:40.851 --> 00:48:43.820
- This is what I would, this is what—
- Yeah, like, if they were watching me, like,

00:48:44.021 --> 00:48:46.590
how would I want to be right now? Like,
what would I want them to see?

00:48:46.757 --> 00:48:48.458
I've done it with different people
through the years.

00:48:48.458 --> 00:48:49.960
They're just,
they're just right now No. 1 on the list.

00:48:49.960 --> 00:48:53.630
- Who's, like, the funniest person you've done it with?
- Um, funniest.

00:48:54.464 --> 00:48:55.499
- Or most interesting.

00:48:55.499 --> 00:48:56.967
- Yeah. Um, no,

00:48:56.967 --> 00:48:59.236
there's been moments where it's been like,
nobody really like that interesting,

00:48:59.236 --> 00:49:00.070
to be honest.

00:49:00.070 --> 00:49:00.504
- Yeah.

00:49:00.504 --> 00:49:03.674
- Moments where it's like, maybe it's
an Olympic year, so maybe it's coming up.

00:49:03.707 --> 00:49:05.542
It's like, all right,
if the committee was in here, like,

00:49:05.542 --> 00:49:07.844
what would you be doing
if so-and-so were in here, like how would you be,

00:49:07.878 --> 00:49:11.148
you know? Cuz there—listen, we all have
nights where you don't feel like it.

00:49:11.715 --> 00:49:13.450
- Mmhmm.
- We're not different from anybody else.

00:49:13.450 --> 00:49:15.519
But you have to find a way to get over that part.
- Yeah.

00:49:15.919 --> 00:49:18.555
Which leads me to, OK, so—
- I sound crazy.

00:49:18.555 --> 00:49:19.523
- No, you don't.

00:49:19.523 --> 00:49:20.057
I love it.

00:49:20.057 --> 00:49:22.359
I feel like we all kind of think,
like, “What would we do?”

00:49:22.359 --> 00:49:24.761
So I, like, make up scenarios in my head
that are conversations

00:49:24.761 --> 00:49:25.495
I'm never going to have.

00:49:26.563 --> 00:49:27.531
Retirement.

00:49:27.531 --> 00:49:28.465
- Yes, we're here.

00:49:28.465 --> 00:49:30.267
- We're here. We're here.

00:49:30.267 --> 00:49:31.601
When will you know it's the end?

00:49:31.601 --> 00:49:34.972
- Oh, man. I'm in a real weird place.

00:49:37.541 --> 00:49:40.677
Cuz the “one more year” chant,
there was an assumption,

00:49:40.877 --> 00:49:44.448
fair assumption, that there would be
one more year, which implies retirement.

00:49:44.448 --> 00:49:48.986
And there are moments where I'm like,
yeah, this feels like the right—

00:49:48.986 --> 00:49:50.487
this could be the last year.

00:49:50.487 --> 00:49:52.923
It feels right for it to be the last year.

00:49:52.923 --> 00:49:55.392
And I explained to you
kind of how the season ended

00:49:55.392 --> 00:49:59.262
and how I was really tired. And the way
I'm feeling right now is I'm not so tired.

00:49:59.262 --> 00:50:01.231
And I actually feel
pretty good physically.

00:50:01.231 --> 00:50:03.633
So that's messing with my head
a little bit.

00:50:03.633 --> 00:50:07.771
So I do think once the season starts—
and I know it's not, like, fair.

00:50:08.338 --> 00:50:10.874
I'm not trying to, like, Tom Brady
or nothing like that.

00:50:10.907 --> 00:50:13.477
You know, it's not, like, necessarily fair.
- Yeah.

00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:17.147
- Cuz I know a lot is being built around
this potentially being my last year,

00:50:17.447 --> 00:50:18.582
which I already know I'm going to like,

00:50:18.582 --> 00:50:21.051
Day 1 of training camp
team, team-only meeting,

00:50:21.051 --> 00:50:22.419
I'm going to be like, “Guys,

00:50:22.419 --> 00:50:24.554
I'm really sorry that there's
going to be a lot of questions.”

00:50:24.755 --> 00:50:27.357
- Yeah.
- Like, I'm going to have to have that conversation

00:50:28.592 --> 00:50:30.894
cuz I don’t want to be a distraction
or an annoyance for anybody.

00:50:31.395 --> 00:50:35.565
But yeah, so I think very quickly, like
within the first like month, two months,

00:50:35.565 --> 00:50:38.568
I think I'll have an idea
cuz the grind of the season,

00:50:38.568 --> 00:50:41.038
you can't really, you know, imitate that

00:50:41.605 --> 00:50:45.208
in your offseason, and
that'll be the story of where I am.

00:50:45.208 --> 00:50:46.877
So I think I'll know pretty quickly.

00:50:46.877 --> 00:50:49.980
So when you made that decision,
you know, that you weren't going to retire,

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:52.282
and you said you went
and he spoke to your physical trainer,

00:50:52.315 --> 00:50:54.184
what was that conversation?

00:50:54.184 --> 00:50:56.253
- So basically—her name's Susan.

00:50:56.253 --> 00:50:57.020
She's amazing.

00:50:57.020 --> 00:50:58.755
And I was basically like, “All right,

00:50:58.755 --> 00:51:01.792
Susan, be real with me.”
There's zero—

00:51:01.825 --> 00:51:02.859
I'm a person.

00:51:02.859 --> 00:51:04.227
I like to live in reality.

00:51:04.227 --> 00:51:05.729
Like, do not sugarcoat.

00:51:05.729 --> 00:51:07.397
I don't work well with that.
- Right.

00:51:08.565 --> 00:51:09.066
- Give it to me

00:51:09.066 --> 00:51:09.599
real.

00:51:09.599 --> 00:51:14.671
I was like, I feel like, you know,
this isn't as strong as it could be.

00:51:14.671 --> 00:51:17.808
I feel like I can't, like, decelerate,

00:51:17.808 --> 00:51:21.078
like, slow down in the ways that I need to
to play.

00:51:21.078 --> 00:51:22.379
Like, I could feel getting—

00:51:22.379 --> 00:51:24.648
I feel like a lot of times
you get a little, like, tippy-toe

00:51:25.015 --> 00:51:27.150
because you can't like [beeping noises] as fast.
- Yeah.

00:51:27.150 --> 00:51:29.019
- Like, tippy-toe, tippy-toe around the place.

00:51:29.019 --> 00:51:31.455
I feel like
I'm tippy-toeing a little bit too much.

00:51:32.022 --> 00:51:33.256
So I just got like real nitty-gritty.

00:51:33.256 --> 00:51:35.992
I was like,
these are the things that I feel

00:51:36.526 --> 00:51:39.096
are, have taken a turn for the worse.

00:51:39.496 --> 00:51:40.430
Are they fixable?

00:51:40.430 --> 00:51:42.833
Or is, like, this is just what it is?

00:51:42.833 --> 00:51:44.167
And that's OK too.

00:51:44.167 --> 00:51:45.735
And she was like, “They’re fixable.”

00:51:45.735 --> 00:51:47.737
And I was like, “Well, let's go, then.”

00:51:47.737 --> 00:51:50.507
And that's kind of been the whole, like,

00:51:50.507 --> 00:51:54.111
basis or foundation of my
offseason has been trying

00:51:54.111 --> 00:51:58.014
to, you know, for lack of a better
fix or improve in these areas

00:51:59.483 --> 00:51:59.816
because

00:51:59.816 --> 00:52:02.953
those were the reasons—I was also,
there is the fatigue part,

00:52:02.953 --> 00:52:04.921
but, like, that's what I was feeling.

00:52:04.921 --> 00:52:06.656
And she was like,
“Oh, no, these are definitely fixable.”

00:52:06.656 --> 00:52:07.991
- Yeah. Like, we can make this work.

00:52:07.991 --> 00:52:11.895
- Like, we can make this better.
- So the end—and I’m not trying to put words in your mouth—

00:52:12.028 --> 00:52:15.165
but it seems like the end would come
when the things

00:52:15.165 --> 00:52:17.400
that are happening in
your life cannot be fixed.

00:52:18.201 --> 00:52:21.238
- Maybe, yeah.
- Like if it's, if it's these physical
things that are like,

00:52:21.238 --> 00:52:23.106
this is gonna be a really hard grind,

00:52:23.106 --> 00:52:25.842
it's like, well,
I don't know if—but then that's also like,

00:52:26.610 --> 00:52:28.111
what if they can always be fixed?

00:52:28.111 --> 00:52:29.579
How much longer?

00:52:29.579 --> 00:52:31.681
- Yeah, that is where the problem lies.

00:52:31.681 --> 00:52:33.016
- Yeah.

00:52:33.016 --> 00:52:36.920
- I'm sitting here feeling so lucky.

00:52:37.154 --> 00:52:40.223
My teammate Lauren Jackson,

00:52:41.057 --> 00:52:45.962
she retired in…what ended up being
her last season was 2012. And

00:52:47.030 --> 00:52:48.231
she's only a year older.

00:52:48.231 --> 00:52:49.199
She's a year younger than me.

00:52:49.199 --> 00:52:52.302
Sorry. She played much longer.
Like, in basketball years,

00:52:52.302 --> 00:52:53.136
she's like 100.

00:52:53.136 --> 00:52:55.839
- Yeah.
- Cuz she started
when she was 15, professionally.

00:52:57.407 --> 00:52:59.476
But one of the best players of all time

00:52:59.476 --> 00:53:02.479
and had it taken away because of injury.
Physically couldn't do it anymore.

00:53:02.479 --> 00:53:05.715
And I watched her go through that,
and at her retirement ceremony

00:53:05.715 --> 00:53:06.917
she came to Seattle.

00:53:06.917 --> 00:53:10.086
We spent some time together, and listening
to her talk about that and what that was,

00:53:11.087 --> 00:53:15.091
it was like, woof, man, to have it taken away
when you're not ready.

00:53:15.625 --> 00:53:18.762
So I mean, so I'm sitting here very lucky.

00:53:21.198 --> 00:53:23.600
But, you know, it's one of those things
where they're both true.

00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:26.136
The other thing that's hard
is when you still physically can.

00:53:26.636 --> 00:53:28.672
And so now you have
this other kind of decision.

00:53:29.573 --> 00:53:32.342
So what's worse: having it taken away—
I think that's worse.

00:53:32.342 --> 00:53:33.677
I shouldn't even phrase it that way.

00:53:33.677 --> 00:53:35.679
I guess what I'm saying
is like, no decision,

00:53:35.679 --> 00:53:38.248
but it's a bad,
or you have to make the decision.

00:53:38.248 --> 00:53:39.015
It's on you.

00:53:39.015 --> 00:53:39.983
And that's how I feel now.

00:53:39.983 --> 00:53:44.688
So I'm lucky, but I know that's part
of—that's where the answer is

00:53:44.688 --> 00:53:45.889
and it's tough to get there.
- Yeah.

00:53:45.889 --> 00:53:48.858
Like, you're battling with the idea
that what you have is choice.

00:53:49.226 --> 00:53:51.561
- Yeah.
- So stopping is on you.
- Yeah.

00:53:51.928 --> 00:53:54.598
- And continuing to go is on you.
- Yeah.

00:53:54.598 --> 00:53:55.999
- So it’s like, which one do I pick?

00:53:55.999 --> 00:53:57.033
- I don't know. Maybe

00:53:57.033 --> 00:53:58.935
what I'm hoping is that maybe something

00:53:58.935 --> 00:54:01.338
outside of basketball starts
speaking to me so much

00:54:01.571 --> 00:54:03.707
where it's like,
OK, you don't need the game anymore.

00:54:03.707 --> 00:54:05.041
You know, like, there's other things.

00:54:05.041 --> 00:54:07.811
And, like, I definitely feel that creeping
in too. Like, other things interest me.

00:54:07.844 --> 00:54:10.547
- Well, because I was going to ask,
is there any part of you

00:54:10.547 --> 00:54:12.882
that is scared that you almost, like,
don't know who you are

00:54:12.916 --> 00:54:15.218
if you're not playing basketball?
- Identity? Yeah.

00:54:15.418 --> 00:54:19.356
I mean, there's a reason that whole
saying, like, athletes die two deaths, right?

00:54:19.389 --> 00:54:22.726
The one where you actually die
and then the one where you retire.

00:54:22.726 --> 00:54:24.027
- Yeah.
- Cuz It's like the death of an

00:54:25.128 --> 00:54:25.695
identity.

00:54:25.695 --> 00:54:26.796
Yeah. I mean, it plays a role.

00:54:26.796 --> 00:54:31.101
I think what I've really tried to do
since I stopped playing overseas,

00:54:31.101 --> 00:54:33.870
which was now like six, seven,
eight years ago,

00:54:34.671 --> 00:54:38.174
I've really tried to, like,
get out of my comfort zone, branch out,

00:54:38.174 --> 00:54:42.145
try things
cuz I don't want that to happen.

00:54:42.145 --> 00:54:43.546
I think it's gonna happen regardless.

00:54:43.546 --> 00:54:46.716
I remember listening to D-Wade talk
about going to therapy. I started

00:54:46.716 --> 00:54:48.785
seeing a sports psychologist right here
this year.

00:54:48.785 --> 00:54:51.388
I've only had one session, but
we'll get to the second one soon.

00:54:51.388 --> 00:54:53.056
- OK. Yes.
- Yeah.
- Good. I love it.

00:54:53.056 --> 00:54:53.757
That’s important.

00:54:53.757 --> 00:54:54.891
- Very important.

00:54:56.526 --> 00:54:58.695
Cuz it's a lot, you know? Both

00:54:58.695 --> 00:55:01.798
this season, it's a lot to juggle,
and then the whole retirement part.

00:55:01.798 --> 00:55:04.668
So I know it's going to be hard
regardless,

00:55:04.668 --> 00:55:06.102
but I've been trying to set myself up

00:55:06.102 --> 00:55:09.906
where I do have something to
then kind of segue into.

00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:13.076
- Could we see Coach Sue?

00:55:13.076 --> 00:55:14.811
- Uh, I don't know.

00:55:14.811 --> 00:55:16.146
It seems kinda hard.

00:55:16.146 --> 00:55:16.613
- Yeah.

00:55:16.613 --> 00:55:19.282
- I'm not gonna lie.
- It does feel sort of hard.
- It seems kinda hard.

00:55:21.217 --> 00:55:21.618
My

00:55:21.618 --> 00:55:24.020
answer is if it was just coaching,

00:55:25.288 --> 00:55:27.290
I would love to, but it's not.

00:55:27.290 --> 00:55:30.160
There's a lot that goes into that.
A lot of politics, a lot of

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:31.394
I mean, we just had a whole thing
about Coach Auriemma

00:55:31.394 --> 00:55:32.295
and Candace Parker.

00:55:32.295 --> 00:55:34.698
I'm not trying to be that person.

00:55:35.031 --> 00:55:38.034
So yeah, but the actual coaching part,
yeah, it definitely speaks to me.

00:55:38.034 --> 00:55:38.335
- Yeah.

00:55:38.335 --> 00:55:40.003
- But I feel like I just lived this life.

00:55:40.003 --> 00:55:41.404
Do I want to live it again?

00:55:41.404 --> 00:55:42.872
That's kind of,

00:55:42.872 --> 00:55:44.741
that's where it loses me a little bit.

00:55:44.741 --> 00:55:47.944
- Also, you know, new arena in Seattle.

00:55:48.078 --> 00:55:48.945
- So good.

00:55:48.945 --> 00:55:51.648
- Are we going to get a Sue Bird statue?

00:55:53.550 --> 00:55:54.918
- I don’t know. Leave that to the mayor.

00:55:54.918 --> 00:55:56.119
Leave that to the mayor.

00:55:56.119 --> 00:55:58.254
- I know there's maybe some, some mumblings.

00:55:58.254 --> 00:56:02.025
- Are you privy to
if that's a thing that will happen?

00:56:02.025 --> 00:56:04.094
- No, I'm not privy.
I don't want to be in that conversation.

00:56:04.527 --> 00:56:06.863
- You're like, “No, no, no, Taylor. No.”
- I'm good.

00:56:06.863 --> 00:56:09.165
- OK. I think we get a Sue Bird statue.

00:56:09.199 --> 00:56:10.834
So I mean, I think we have to, right?

00:56:10.834 --> 00:56:13.670
I mean, I don't know how you can do this
without one.

00:56:14.104 --> 00:56:16.473
She doesn't want to say,
but I'll say I think that there totally

00:56:16.840 --> 00:56:18.241
should be a statue.

00:56:18.241 --> 00:56:23.213
I do wanna go back to Lauren Jackson
really quick because I know

00:56:23.246 --> 00:56:26.816
when you first got to Seattle,
not friends.

00:56:27.317 --> 00:56:29.085
- Nope.
- She did not like you.
- She did not like me.

00:56:29.085 --> 00:56:30.520
- Did you like her?

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:31.621
- Yeah.
- OK.

00:56:31.621 --> 00:56:34.023
But you understood
she didn't like you.
- Yeah.
- You're new kid in town.

00:56:34.023 --> 00:56:37.293
- Well, she didn't actually verbally say it
till the next year, but, like,

00:56:37.293 --> 00:56:38.328
I kinda caught the drift.

00:56:38.328 --> 00:56:41.131
- Yeah.
- I was like, "Mm, you're not fuckin' with me.
What the fuck?"

00:56:42.732 --> 00:56:46.703
- But—[laughing]. Yeah.
- Like—
- Yeah, she's like, "You hate me."

00:56:47.237 --> 00:56:49.472
- Really.
- But that's interesting—
- Not inviting me places, what is going on—

00:56:49.472 --> 00:56:50.607
No, no, no.
- Yeah. OK, I'm like, oh—

00:56:50.607 --> 00:56:51.908
- But that was the vibe.
- Yeah.

00:56:51.908 --> 00:56:52.976
- The vibe was like, eh.

00:56:53.176 --> 00:56:56.446
- But it's interesting because it clicked
automatically on the court.
- Yes.

00:56:56.446 --> 00:56:58.348
That part was no problem.
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:56:58.348 --> 00:57:03.286
- And I think about how many people
in all sports

00:57:03.486 --> 00:57:07.056
don't actually like each other.
- No. A lot,
- But they make it work.

00:57:07.290 --> 00:57:08.558
What is that like?

00:57:08.558 --> 00:57:11.528
- So this is why athletes

00:57:11.761 --> 00:57:14.631
deserve—more than deserve—

00:57:14.631 --> 00:57:17.867
but deserve
to speak on larger issues in our society.

00:57:18.868 --> 00:57:20.804
This is what we do.

00:57:20.870 --> 00:57:24.107
We sit in rooms with people
we might not like, and we figure it the fuck

00:57:24.107 --> 00:57:28.845
out. For the common good. For everyone.
For everyone to feel good about it.

00:57:29.045 --> 00:57:29.746
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:57:29.746 --> 00:57:32.549
- To achieve, you know, obviously
in my world, it's, like, to achieve

00:57:32.549 --> 00:57:35.585
the championship,
but maybe it's to, like, achieve equality.

00:57:36.319 --> 00:57:38.354
- Yeah.
- This is why.
- Totally.

00:57:38.721 --> 00:57:40.924
- Cuz, like, there's a lotta times
where you don't like each other.

00:57:41.458 --> 00:57:45.028
- Mmhmm.
- And, like, I'm not saying
sports is, like, doesn't get messy.

00:57:45.028 --> 00:57:48.531
- Of course. There are fights both,
you know, verbal and physical.

00:57:48.798 --> 00:57:51.134
I get—and, like, you know,
those are blasted all over the media.

00:57:51.134 --> 00:57:51.868
It exists.

00:57:51.868 --> 00:57:52.135
- Yeah.

00:57:52.135 --> 00:57:55.004
- But at the end of the day, you're
kind of the jerk when you do that.

00:57:55.572 --> 00:57:55.805
- Yeah.

00:57:55.805 --> 00:57:57.807
- You're the asshole in the room

00:57:57.807 --> 00:57:59.576
when you're not putting the team first.
- Mmhmm.

00:57:59.576 --> 00:58:01.678
- And that is how we exist. Like,
that is how we all—

00:58:01.678 --> 00:58:03.813
that's how that ecosystem works in sports.

00:58:03.813 --> 00:58:04.714
And that is always my answer.

00:58:04.714 --> 00:58:07.217
Like, that is why
we get to speak on these things.

00:58:07.217 --> 00:58:09.252
- Yeah.
- Cuz we have microphones in our face, so.

00:58:09.252 --> 00:58:12.789
- Like, are you gonna
ever, like, run for anything?
- No. For office? No.

00:58:13.790 --> 00:58:15.525
- [Laughing] She's like, "I'm ending that
conversation—"

00:58:15.525 --> 00:58:17.627
- Absolutely not.
- "Before you even ask me, OK?"

00:58:17.994 --> 00:58:18.261
Yeah, no—

00:58:18.261 --> 00:58:19.996
- Seriously, that's always my—
- Totally.

00:58:19.996 --> 00:58:22.165
- Yeah.
- And it's true.
- It's so true.

00:58:22.198 --> 00:58:22.499
- Yeah.

00:58:22.499 --> 00:58:23.600
I mean, I think in a lot of ways,

00:58:23.600 --> 00:58:26.736
though, sports kind of
are like a microcosm of society.

00:58:26.736 --> 00:58:29.539
- Yeah.
- And there's a lot of—
- People are like, "Oh, I don't see color."

00:58:29.806 --> 00:58:30.373
- Yeah. It's like—

00:58:30.373 --> 00:58:31.875
- Uh, yeah, you do.
- Yeah.

00:58:31.875 --> 00:58:33.710
- But, like,
that's the thing about every locker room

00:58:33.710 --> 00:58:35.845
I'm in. It's like—
that's the thing. You walk in.

00:58:35.979 --> 00:58:37.146
Of course you see it.

00:58:37.146 --> 00:58:40.550
You have eyeballs. Like, you're seeing it,
but you don't care. Or, like,

00:58:40.984 --> 00:58:45.054
you respect people,
you know, or you have tough conversations.

00:58:45.054 --> 00:58:47.490
Oh, there's been a lot of tough
conversations in our locker rooms.
- Yeah.

00:58:49.359 --> 00:58:49.792
- And it's like,

00:58:49.792 --> 00:58:53.129
but because you have a respect,
you're able to have the tough conversation.
- Mmhmm.

00:58:53.596 --> 00:58:56.866
- And, like, for lack of a better, like,
get to the bottom of it or figure it out

00:58:56.866 --> 00:59:01.304
or come to a good place or, you know, understand.
- Yeah.
- Ugh.
- No, I love it.

00:59:01.671 --> 00:59:02.839
I'm into it.

00:59:02.839 --> 00:59:05.441
I—you know,
so you probably don't remember this,

00:59:05.441 --> 00:59:07.544
but the first time I met you,
it was at this, like,

00:59:07.544 --> 00:59:10.847
Uninterrupted event that we were all doing.
- Oh, yeah.
- And we spoke.

00:59:10.847 --> 00:59:12.315
And I remember I came up to you.
I was like, "I'm such a huge fan.

00:59:12.315 --> 00:59:15.752
Can we take a picture?" Like, you are—even
before, like, we were friends,

00:59:15.752 --> 00:59:18.788
I was like,
"This is one of my favorite sports people."

00:59:18.788 --> 00:59:22.425
It's like, you,
Candace Parker and Diana Taurasi.

00:59:23.126 --> 00:59:27.597
I only met Diana Taurasi once, actually,
at the dinner, Super Bowl week in L.A.

00:59:28.064 --> 00:59:30.600
- OK, right.
- Yes.
- Oh, you were there for, like—
- Yeah, for like 10 seconds.

00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:32.969
I had to go to a party, but—
- [Laughing]

00:59:34.637 --> 00:59:35.471
You did. I can—
- Yes.
- You did.

00:59:35.638 --> 00:59:39.409
- I was ready. I was looking good.
- She was ready. She was looking good.
You were looking good.

00:59:39.943 --> 00:59:44.314
- But tell me a story about Diana Taurasi

00:59:44.647 --> 00:59:47.116
that is, like, indicative
of her personality.

00:59:47.283 --> 00:59:50.453
Like, you tell someone this story
about Diana, you're like, "And that's

00:59:50.453 --> 00:59:57.026
how Diana Taurasi acts."
- Oh, my God. I don't even know
where to go with this. Um…oh, my God.

00:59:58.161 --> 00:59:59.963
There's too many and…ugh.

01:00:01.431 --> 01:00:04.734
Indicative of her personality.

01:00:04.767 --> 01:00:07.971
Um, I don't—

01:00:09.005 --> 01:00:12.275
there's, like, some that I can't say.
- [Laughing]
- That's what I'm struggling with.

01:00:12.275 --> 01:00:16.179
I basically just thought of 10, and
like nine-and-a-half of them, I can't say.

01:00:16.512 --> 01:00:22.218
- But to be fair though—
- That would be—
- That actually tells you what you need to know about her personality, too.

01:00:22.218 --> 01:00:25.021
- But, like, also,
if I did tell them, you'd be like, "Yep."

01:00:25.588 --> 01:00:28.157
- Yeah.
- Or, like, laugh, or—you would never—

01:00:28.191 --> 01:00:29.792
very rarely—

01:00:29.792 --> 01:00:32.395
I feel like only
when she's maybe, like, arguing with refs

01:00:32.395 --> 01:00:34.931
do you disagree with her. You're kinda like,
"Oh, yeah, you

01:00:35.064 --> 01:00:37.367
didn't foul," or "Yeah, you
got fouled," you know?

01:00:38.267 --> 01:00:39.068
- Yeah.
- But no, she, um…

01:00:40.036 --> 01:00:41.871
man, I'll–let me—maybe once—
yeah.

01:00:41.871 --> 01:00:44.207
- Okay, I'll come back to talk about it.

01:00:44.207 --> 01:00:48.211
Before we get to the fan
question section of this,

01:00:48.211 --> 01:00:52.382
I cannot go through this interview
without talking about a conversation

01:00:52.382 --> 01:00:54.517
that you and I had
that always sticks with me.

01:00:55.818 --> 01:00:58.254
Something you've talked about a lot in
this interview is Megan

01:00:58.254 --> 01:01:01.157
and just the impact
obviously she has had on your life.

01:01:01.891 --> 01:01:05.995
If anybody wonders to themselves,
like, "Hmm, do Sue and

01:01:05.995 --> 01:01:09.932
Megan really act like what it seems like
they do online," the answer is yes.

01:01:09.932 --> 01:01:14.804
Like, I have been around them so many times,
and the love that you guys have

01:01:14.804 --> 01:01:18.975
is just, like, so real and tangible,
and you're best friends.

01:01:18.975 --> 01:01:21.978
It's like you just know
everything about each other.

01:01:21.978 --> 01:01:24.380
Like, you just know each other
like the, like the back of your hands.

01:01:24.847 --> 01:01:26.349
But we were talking,

01:01:26.349 --> 01:01:30.019
and I said, "You know, a lot of times when
we're talking about relationships and love,

01:01:30.153 --> 01:01:33.723
we are asked, like, why we love that person,

01:01:34.090 --> 01:01:37.827
but we're very rarely asked
why we think they love us."

01:01:37.860 --> 01:01:38.528
- Yeah.

01:01:38.528 --> 01:01:40.363
- So why do you think—
- I'm like, "What did I say?"

01:01:40.363 --> 01:01:43.232
- Yeah [laughing]. Why do you think Megan loves you?
- There's a lot of reasons.

01:01:43.332 --> 01:01:46.903
- And remind me what I said though.
- You were talking about just like the,

01:01:47.036 --> 01:01:49.739
almost like the peace that you feel like you bring.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. That's what I thought. OK.

01:01:51.107 --> 01:01:53.476
- Yeah.
- Um, yeah. So,

01:01:53.476 --> 01:01:55.945
I think what I have

01:01:55.945 --> 01:01:59.415
brought to Megan's
life is, like, a sense of calm.

01:01:59.415 --> 01:02:01.951
And I think—and that goes both ways.

01:02:02.852 --> 01:02:05.621
We often say that when we met, we,

01:02:06.856 --> 01:02:09.125
like, started talking
and just never stopped.

01:02:09.125 --> 01:02:11.294
Like,
a conversation started. It never stopped.

01:02:11.627 --> 01:02:15.765
And I think in that conversation,
if you remember, we started dating early—

01:02:15.765 --> 01:02:19.469
I'm sorry, late 2016.
Fall of 2016.

01:02:21.437 --> 01:02:23.272
She'd knelt,

01:02:23.339 --> 01:02:26.142
like, a week or two
after we started talking.

01:02:26.542 --> 01:02:28.010
I don't remember the exact timeline.

01:02:28.010 --> 01:02:28.377
- Yeah.

01:02:28.377 --> 01:02:30.646
- She was also engaged and—ugh.
- It was messy.

01:02:30.913 --> 01:02:31.380
- It was eh.
- Yeah [laughing].

01:02:32.582 --> 01:02:35.118
You're like, "Uh…"
- Fast forward, fast forward, fast forward.

01:02:35.618 --> 01:02:38.187
But it was at a—
- [Laughing]

01:02:38.421 --> 01:02:39.155
- Skip, skip, skip.

01:02:39.222 --> 01:02:42.458
It was at a time when—I say
that with respect.

01:02:42.525 --> 01:02:45.361
- Yes.
- It was at a time when

01:02:45.728 --> 01:02:48.064
she had a lot going on.
- Yeah.

01:02:48.064 --> 01:02:50.266
- I mean, as you can imagine. And I think

01:02:52.135 --> 01:02:54.604
she was—one of the byproducts of that

01:02:54.604 --> 01:02:57.607
moment was not being called
into the national team

01:02:57.607 --> 01:03:00.743
camps.
- Mmhmm.
- A lot of byproducts, but that was one of them.

01:03:00.743 --> 01:03:02.011
And there's this one moment

01:03:02.011 --> 01:03:04.881
where, that we joke about now,
but it was like, she wrote this, like,

01:03:05.348 --> 01:03:08.951
seething crazy email,
and she was about to send it to the

01:03:08.951 --> 01:03:13.723
then-coach, and I was like, "Well, like,
maybe we need to just take a second."

01:03:13.723 --> 01:03:14.090
- Yeah.

01:03:14.090 --> 01:03:18.928
- And I think that Megan—what makes Megan
so wonderful and what makes Megan

01:03:18.928 --> 01:03:22.265
Megan is this, like, just like,

01:03:22.398 --> 01:03:25.568
first of all, supreme confidence.
- Totally.
- Never gets embarrassed.

01:03:25.868 --> 01:03:26.936
It's impossible.
- Mmhmm.

01:03:26.936 --> 01:03:29.138
- You can try. She just does not get embarrassed.
- Yeah.

01:03:31.874 --> 01:03:34.043
- And on top of that, like—

01:03:34.043 --> 01:03:37.547
I just lost my train
of thought. On top of that, her—

01:03:38.581 --> 01:03:40.716
how do I wanna word this? Like,
her—

01:03:40.716 --> 01:03:42.585
she follows her instincts, right?

01:03:42.585 --> 01:03:44.053
And just, boom, goes with it.

01:03:44.053 --> 01:03:47.456
Doesn't think about it, doesn't question
it, just goes. I'm the exact opposite.

01:03:47.790 --> 01:03:49.192
I, like, suf—oh, my God,

01:03:49.192 --> 01:03:51.894
decision fatigue times a thousand.
- Yeah.
- I, like, cannot—

01:03:51.894 --> 01:03:54.197
I mean, I had to choose a flight
to go to the Final Four, and I was like,

01:03:54.697 --> 01:03:57.033
"9:00 or 11:00? 9:00 or 11:00?
9:00 or 11:00?!"

01:03:57.033 --> 01:04:00.636
I'm like, I couldn't choose.
- Yeah.

01:04:00.636 --> 01:04:02.104
- And I think that balance

01:04:02.104 --> 01:04:06.175
for us, like, us together is perfect.
- Mmhmm.
- Cuz there is me,

01:04:06.175 --> 01:04:08.845
who's gonna, like, weigh it out. Her,
who's just gonna do it.

01:04:09.245 --> 01:04:12.114
And together we have
found this, like, amazing balance.

01:04:12.114 --> 01:04:16.385
And I think with that, one of the things
that comes from that is this, like, calm.

01:04:16.586 --> 01:04:18.588
- Yeah.
- It's peaceful. And I think

01:04:19.589 --> 01:04:21.958
there's many ways I
can go in the calm, peaceful conversation.

01:04:21.991 --> 01:04:23.159
For some reason, I chose that route.

