- It kind of has the sauce on it, though.
- OK.

01:15:17.713 --> 01:15:18.881
- It's good.
- It's good? OK.
- Yeah.

01:15:18.881 --> 01:15:22.017
And then a little hack is going
there breakfast time and getting

01:15:22.017 --> 01:15:26.054
just, like, the egg and cheese biscuit but
asking them to put chicken on it.
- On top of it?

01:15:26.054 --> 01:15:29.091
- Well, a chicken biscuit for breakfast.
- Is this, like, a JFK terminal situation?

01:15:29.091 --> 01:15:31.260
- Yes, literally
that one right across from the gate.

01:15:31.293 --> 01:15:32.427
Hundred percent.
- [Laughing]

01:15:32.427 --> 01:15:33.228
- I get it

01:15:33.228 --> 01:15:35.797
all the time from there
when I have to, like, fly to L.A.

01:15:35.797 --> 01:15:37.866
early in the morning
and I need some breakfast.

01:15:37.900 --> 01:15:38.834
- We all know Terminal 4.

01:15:38.834 --> 01:15:41.436
- Yes.
- [Laughing]
- Klay put his question in.

01:15:41.703 --> 01:15:44.706
- OK.
- We, I know we're doing fan questions.
We have a question from Klay Thompson.

01:15:45.641 --> 01:15:47.843
"As an ocean enthusiast,

01:15:48.043 --> 01:15:51.346
where is the best
spot for seafood in the 206?"

01:15:52.714 --> 01:15:54.249
- You want to give yours a shout?

01:15:54.249 --> 01:15:56.451
- RockCreek. Shoutout RockCreek. Went there last night.

01:15:56.451 --> 01:15:58.487
It's very good. Go get that mussels
bruschetta.

01:15:58.487 --> 01:15:59.588
Thank you very much.

01:15:59.588 --> 01:16:01.823
What about for you?

01:16:01.823 --> 01:16:05.294
- I would say…if you want, I would say, mhm, this is tough.
Cuz you can go,

01:16:05.294 --> 01:16:10.899
like, classic, like, you know,
oysters, crab, that kind of a vibe.

01:16:11.233 --> 01:16:12.034
There's a bunch of places like that.

01:16:12.034 --> 01:16:14.436
But there's this one place
called Walrus and The Carpenter.

01:16:14.703 --> 01:16:17.573
If you like oysters, Klay,
this is the spot.

01:16:18.807 --> 01:16:20.842
And they do a bunch of other, like, fun
dishes.

01:16:20.842 --> 01:16:24.012
They actually have a fried oyster that's
bomb,
- Ooh.
- a bunch of other seafood dishes.

01:16:24.012 --> 01:16:24.546
They have other things

01:16:24.546 --> 01:16:27.816
besides seafood, but oysters
and kind of, like, a quintessential Seattle,

01:16:28.216 --> 01:16:30.552
kind of, like, chill vibe.
Walrus and The Carpenter.

01:16:30.686 --> 01:16:31.887
- OK. I love that.

01:16:31.887 --> 01:16:36.425
And he has a second question that I'm
not going to ask cuz you addressed it.

01:16:36.425 --> 01:16:40.662
He said, "How is she able to play decades
at such a high level, surviving the wear

01:16:40.662 --> 01:16:42.197
and tear of season after season?"

01:16:42.197 --> 01:16:45.834
But the reason I'm telling you is
I think it it really speaks

01:16:45.834 --> 01:16:48.670
to what everybody sees in you,
- Yeah.

01:16:49.204 --> 01:16:52.040
- which is that she's still doing this,

01:16:52.674 --> 01:16:56.612
and it's still working, and she's still, like,
one of the greatest.

01:16:56.612 --> 01:17:00.682
And I just want to take a minute
for that, like, gravity to really sit in.

01:17:01.116 --> 01:17:03.318
Because something I always think about
is this interview that

01:17:03.885 --> 01:17:05.087
Denzel Washington did.

01:17:05.087 --> 01:17:08.256
And someone asked him,
"What are you most proud of in your career?"

01:17:08.624 --> 01:17:10.258
And he said, "The career."

01:17:10.258 --> 01:17:10.726
- Yeah.

01:17:10.726 --> 01:17:14.296
- Like, I'm proud of the longevity and the things
that I was able to do within it.

01:17:14.663 --> 01:17:17.566
And I have seen so many interviews
where you're talking about, like,

01:17:18.033 --> 01:17:19.935
I feel like I have something to prove.

01:17:19.935 --> 01:17:22.671
Like, even now, I'm, like, wanting to prove things.

01:17:23.038 --> 01:17:26.875
So with this existing, this, like, great
longevity that you've had, the respect

01:17:26.875 --> 01:17:31.747
that you have amongst your peers, what more
could you possibly have to prove?

01:17:31.780 --> 01:17:32.147
- Yeah,

01:17:34.316 --> 01:17:34.750
so there's,

01:17:34.750 --> 01:17:38.020
I feel like there's always in the back of my head

01:17:38.020 --> 01:17:41.590
this, like, proving that this isn't a fluke,
right?

01:17:41.623 --> 01:17:45.961
Like, proving that, you know,
there's a reason or proving—

01:17:46.461 --> 01:17:49.865
But I have to be honest, I think it's, like,
starting to shift, which is probably

01:17:49.865 --> 01:17:53.669
also like, whoop, time to retire,
but it is starting to shift a little in that—

01:17:54.036 --> 01:17:57.105
and this is when I usually quote
or use one of Kobe's quotes,

01:17:57.105 --> 01:18:01.109
which was talking about legacy
and speaking about how, I don't remember

01:18:01.109 --> 01:18:04.513
the exact quote, but he was basically
saying there's gonna be another MVP.

01:18:04.513 --> 01:18:05.013
There's gonna be—

01:18:05.013 --> 01:18:07.816
and so for me, it's, like, someone's
going to break my assist record.

01:18:08.216 --> 01:18:10.485
There's gonna come a point guard
that wins more championships

01:18:10.485 --> 01:18:12.654
and does more things. Like, it's just,
this is what it is.

01:18:12.654 --> 01:18:14.456
This is sports. It's evolution. That's cool.

01:18:15.524 --> 01:18:17.325
So what is legacy really? Like,

01:18:17.325 --> 01:18:18.794
what else do I have to prove, really?

01:18:18.794 --> 01:18:21.496
And we touched, we talked about it earlier

01:18:21.496 --> 01:18:23.932
when you were saying, like,
is there the guilt that comes in?

01:18:23.932 --> 01:18:26.501
And I think that's kind of where
my legacy lives now.

01:18:26.501 --> 01:18:28.136
Like, what can I do

01:18:28.136 --> 01:18:32.207
both from a bask—like, specifically a
WNBA basketball standpoint to make sure

01:18:32.774 --> 01:18:36.445
this league is, like, in a good place
and it's, you know, not just surviving

01:18:36.445 --> 01:18:38.280
but killing it. Like, what can I do?

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:41.550
And I feel like I can share my experience,
and I can speak up.

01:18:42.584 --> 01:18:45.087
Another part of it is, like,
how can I help

01:18:45.087 --> 01:18:46.755
create more opportunities?

01:18:46.755 --> 01:18:50.225
And so it's, like, with that guilt,
I just know at the same time

01:18:50.859 --> 01:18:56.098
I can kind of, like, push that to the side
because I know when I am in these rooms,

01:18:56.798 --> 01:18:59.835
I can guarantee you and I can promise,
I guess is a better way of saying it,

01:18:59.835 --> 01:19:03.138
that I am making sure it's
not just me getting this, right?
- Mmhmm.

01:19:03.138 --> 01:19:06.308
- Like, I'm trying to actively,
when I'm in these rooms,

01:19:06.541 --> 01:19:09.277
use my voice to make sure
more opportunities are created.

01:19:09.444 --> 01:19:11.379
So even though it might feel,

01:19:11.379 --> 01:19:14.149
you know, like, one person, one
athlete is getting that, it's, like, that's

01:19:14.149 --> 01:19:17.319
where I feel like I know I can
really help create difference.

01:19:17.319 --> 01:19:18.653
And that is legacy, right?

01:19:18.653 --> 01:19:22.224
That is what my career will also
have connected to it.

01:19:22.924 --> 01:19:24.593
- That's so powerful. I love that.

01:19:24.593 --> 01:19:25.727
Any more fan questions?

01:19:27.496 --> 01:19:29.598
